INSERT INTO `interview_question` VALUES (1,1,'Tell me about a time you worked in a team.','请讲一个你参与团队合作的经历。','I worked on a web application project with several teammates, where we were responsible for different parts of the system. My main responsibility was backend API design and development. We aligned on coding style, project structure, and interface contracts early, which helped us work smoothly and finish the project on time.','I worked on a web application project with several teammates where we were responsible for different parts of the system, including the frontend, app client, and backend. My role was mainly on the backend side, including API design and development.\n\nTo make the teamwork smoother, we first agreed on the project structure, coding style, and task division. I kept regular communication with my teammates, especially when my work depended on their part. For example, before implementing some backend APIs, I discussed the data structure and interface requirements with the frontend teammate to avoid mismatches later. In addition, having the interface contract defined in advance allowed both sides to work in parallel and prevented unnecessary delays in development.\n\nDuring the project, I also helped review integration issues and fixed some problems that affected other team members. In the end, we completed the project on time and the system worked well as a whole.\n\nThis experience taught me that good teamwork is not just about finishing your own tasks. It is also about communication, coordination, and helping the team move forward together.','Focus on collaboration, interface alignment, ownership, and communication across teams.','teamwork, collaboration, backend, api, communication, interface contract',1,1,1,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(2,1,'Tell me about a conflict you had with a teammate.','请讲一个你和队友发生分歧或冲突的经历。','In one cloud drive project, a teammate and I had different opinions about API response design. I wanted to standardize all controller responses with a common wrapper, while he believed some scenarios should return raw data directly. We discussed both viewpoints and finally introduced an annotation-based switch, which became a practical compromise.','In one of our cloud drive projects, I had a difference of opinion with a teammate about API response design.\n\nTo provide a meaningful response of our API, I designed a response format with code, message, and data, which makes the API result much clearer for callers. It tells them whether the request was successful, and if not, it provides the failure reason in a consistent way. So I designed a Spring MVC interceptor to wrap all controller responses into this standard format. We had no conflict about the data structure, but we had different opinions on the implementation approach. I thought this would make our APIs more consistent and reduce repeated work in business code.\n\nMy teammate disagreed because he felt we should not change the original return values of other methods. He also pointed out that some scenarios required returning raw data directly, without any extra wrapper.\n\nSo instead of arguing, we discussed both sides carefully. I explained the benefits of a unified response using an interceptor, and I also agreed that his concern made sense in some cases.\n\nFinally, we came up with a compromise: we added an annotation to control whether a controller method should use the wrapper or return the original data. That solution resolved the disagreement well, and later it was even adopted by other teams in our department.\n\nThis experience taught me that when there is disagreement in a team, the key is to focus on the problem, respect different viewpoints, and work toward a practical solution.','Show that you stayed calm, respected the other person’s concern, and found a practical compromise.','conflict, disagreement, api design, spring mvc, compromise, teamwork',2,1,2,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(3,1,'How do you work with difficult team members?','你如何与难相处的团队成员合作？','When I work with a difficult team member, I try not to take it personally. I focus on understanding the real issue, such as pressure, unclear requirements, or communication style. I keep communication calm and solution-oriented, and I try to align on goals, responsibilities, and deadlines.','When I work with a difficult team member, I try not to take it personally. I first focus on understanding the real issue. Sometimes the problem is not the person, but pressure, unclear requirements, or communication style.\n\nI prefer to keep communication calm and professional. I try to align on goals, responsibilities, and deadlines, so that expectations are clear. If there is still a problem, I focus on facts and solutions instead of emotions.\n\nIn my experience, many teamwork issues can be improved by better communication and mutual understanding. I think being patient, respectful, and solution-oriented is very important in a team environment.','Emphasize emotional maturity, professionalism, and problem-solving instead of blaming others.','difficult team members, communication, patience, professionalism, teamwork',1,1,3,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(4,1,'What role do you usually play in a team?','你在团队中通常扮演什么角色？','I am usually a reliable and collaborative team member. I take ownership of my part, but I also pay attention to how my work connects with others. In technical projects, I often help keep things organized by clarifying requirements, defining interfaces, and communicating risks early.','I am usually a reliable and collaborative team member. I take ownership of my part, but I also pay attention to how my work connects with others.\n\nIn technical projects, I often play the role of someone who helps keep things organized. For example, I like to clarify requirements, define interfaces clearly, and communicate early if I see possible risks or blockers.\n\nI am also happy to support teammates when needed, whether that means helping debug an issue, reviewing code, or discussing implementation ideas. I think a strong team needs people who can both deliver individually and collaborate effectively.','Position yourself as dependable, organized, and supportive, not as someone trying to dominate the team.','team role, reliable, ownership, collaboration, interfaces, communication',1,1,4,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(5,1,'Describe a time when you helped a teammate.','请描述一次你帮助队友的经历。','In one project, a teammate was struggling with an integration issue between the frontend and backend. I helped trace the API response mismatch, explained the backend logic, and adjusted the response structure. After that, the issue was resolved quickly and the teammate could continue work without delay.','In one project, a teammate was struggling with an integration issue between the frontend and backend. The API response format did not match what the frontend expected, so some data could not be displayed correctly.\n\nAlthough it was not entirely my task, I worked with that teammate to trace the issue. I checked the API response, explained the backend logic, and helped adjust the response structure so it was easier to use on the frontend side.\n\nAfter that, the issue was resolved quickly and the teammate could continue their work without further delay. I think helping teammates in this way improves both project efficiency and team trust.','Show willingness to support others, not just finish your own work.','help teammate, integration issue, frontend backend, support, trust',1,1,5,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(6,1,'How do you ensure effective communication in a team?','你如何确保团队中的有效沟通？','I think effective communication depends on clarity, timing, and attitude. I try to communicate clearly about requirements, progress, and blockers. I also raise issues early and communicate respectfully. In technical work, clear documentation and written updates are also very important.','I think effective communication in a team depends on clarity, timing, and attitude.\n\nFirst, I try to communicate clearly, especially about requirements, progress, and blockers. Second, I prefer to raise issues early instead of waiting until they become bigger problems. Third, I try to communicate respectfully and constructively, even when there are disagreements.\n\nIn technical work, I also think written communication is important. Clear comments, documentation, and task updates can reduce misunderstandings and make collaboration smoother.','Mention both verbal and written communication. Show that you raise issues early.','effective communication, clarity, blockers, documentation, teamwork',1,1,6,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(7,1,'Tell me about a time your team failed or faced a challenge.','请讲一个你所在团队遇到失败或挑战的经历。','In one project, our team underestimated the time needed for integration and testing. When we combined everything, we found compatibility issues between modules. I helped the team identify the highest-priority issues first, recheck interfaces, and improve communication. Although the project became more stressful than expected, we still completed the main requirements.','In one project, our team underestimated the time needed for integration and testing. Individually, each part seemed to be progressing well, but when we combined everything, we found several compatibility issues.\n\nAt that stage, I focused on helping the team identify the highest-priority problems first. We rechecked the interfaces between modules, improved communication, and adjusted our task priorities to solve the most critical issues quickly.\n\nAlthough the project became more stressful than expected, we still managed to complete the main requirements. The biggest lesson for me was that teamwork is not only about dividing tasks. It also requires continuous alignment throughout the project.','Choose a challenge with a constructive ending. Focus on what you did to help the team recover.','challenge, integration, testing, priorities, communication, teamwork',2,1,7,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(8,1,'How do you handle working in a diverse team?','你如何在多元化团队中工作？','I am comfortable working in diverse teams. Different backgrounds, skills, and perspectives can make a team stronger. I try to listen carefully, respect different viewpoints, and keep my communication clear and professional.','I am comfortable working in diverse teams. Different backgrounds, skills, and perspectives can make a team stronger.\n\nWhen working in a diverse team, I think it is important to listen carefully, respect different viewpoints, and avoid assuming that everyone communicates in the same way. I try to keep my communication clear and professional, and I am always open to learning from others.\n\nIn my experience, diverse teams often produce better ideas because people bring different ways of thinking to the problem.','Show openness, respect, and adaptability. Avoid sounding generic.','diverse team, respect, listening, communication, adaptability',1,1,8,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(9,1,'Why is teamwork important?','为什么团队合作很重要？','Teamwork is important because most meaningful work is too complex for one person to do alone. In a good team, people bring different skills, experiences, and ideas, which leads to better solutions. Good teamwork also improves efficiency and helps people move toward a common goal.','Teamwork is important because most meaningful work is too complex for one person to do alone. In a good team, people bring different skills, experiences, and ideas, which leads to better solutions.\n\nGood teamwork also improves efficiency. When responsibilities are clear and communication is effective, the team can solve problems faster and deliver better results.\n\nFor me, teamwork is not just about working together. It is about trusting each other, supporting each other, and moving toward a common goal.','Keep it practical: complexity, efficiency, trust, and shared goals.','teamwork importance, collaboration, efficiency, trust, shared goals',1,1,9,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(10,1,'How would you describe your teamwork style in a short interview answer?','如果面试中要简短描述你的团队合作风格，你会怎么说？','I work well in a team because I communicate clearly, take responsibility for my tasks, and try to support others when needed. I believe good teamwork depends on trust, respect, and alignment on shared goals.','I work well in a team because I communicate clearly, take responsibility for my tasks, and try to support others when needed. I believe good teamwork depends on trust, respect, and alignment on shared goals. In my past projects, I found that strong communication and cooperation made a big difference to the final result.','Useful as a concise backup answer. Keep it natural and confident.','short answer, teamwork style, communication, responsibility, support',1,1,10,1,1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16',1,'2026-04-07 14:35:16'),(11,7,'1. What is the difference between JDK, JRE, and JVM?','JDK,JRE和JVM 有什么区别？','JVM is the Java Virtual Machine. It runs Java bytecode.\r\nJRE is the Java Runtime Environment. It includes the JVM plus libraries needed to run Java applications.\r\nJDK is the Java Development Kit. It includes the JRE plus development tools like javac, javadoc, and jdb.\r\n\r\nIn simple terms:\r\n• JVM = executes Java bytecode\r\n• JRE = runs Java programs\r\n• JDK = develops and runs Java programs','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 07:15:36',1,'2026-04-16 07:15:36'),(12,7,'2. What are the differences between an abstract class and an interface?','抽象类和抽象方法有什么区别？具体的使用场景有什么不同','An interface is mainly used to define a contract, while an abstract class is used to provide partial implementation and code reuse.\r\n\r\nAn abstract class can have abstract methods, concrete methods, fields, and constructors. An interface mainly defines behavior, although since Java 8 it can also have default and static methods.\r\n\r\nA class can extend only one abstract class, but interfaces support multiple inheritance.\r\n\r\nI would use an interface for defining common behavior across unrelated classes, and an abstract class when related classes need to share common logic.','An abstract class can contain abstract methods, concrete methods, member variables, constants, and constructors.\r\n\r\nAn interface is mainly used to define a contract. Traditionally, it only contained abstract methods, although since Java 8 it can also have default methods and static methods. It cannot have normal instance fields. Any variables defined in an interface are implicitly public, static, and final, so they are constants.\r\n\r\nAnother difference is inheritance. A class can extend only one abstract class, but an interface can extend multiple interfaces.\r\n\r\nIn terms of usage, an interface is mainly used to define a standard or specification for behavior, rather than to provide shared implementation. An abstract class, on the other hand, is useful when you want to share some common implementation among related subclasses while still leaving some methods abstract for subclasses to implement.\r\n\r\nThis allows different subclasses to reuse existing logic from the abstract class, while still providing their own customized behavior.','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 07:29:55',1,'2026-04-16 07:29:55'),(13,7,'3. What is the difference between == and .equals() in Java?','java中的== 和 equals方法有什么区别','== compares actual values for primitive types, but for objects it compares whether the references point to the same object.\r\n\r\nequals() is used for logical equality. By default, it also compares references, but we can override it to define equality based on business rules. If we override equals(), we should also override hashCode().','In Java, == is used to compare whether two things are the same.\r\n\r\nIf we use it with primitive types, it compares their actual values. For example, for int or double, it checks whether the values are equal.\r\n\r\nIf we use == with reference types, it compares whether the two references point to the same object in memory.\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, equals() is used to compare logical equality between two objects. By default, the implementation in the Object class also compares object references, so without overriding it, it behaves similarly to == for objects.\r\n\r\nHowever, we can override equals() based on business requirements to define what it means for two objects to be considered equal. When we override equals(), we should also override hashCode() to keep them consistent, especially when the object is used in hash-based collections like HashMap or HashSet.','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 07:45:39',1,'2026-04-16 07:45:39'),(14,7,'4. Why must we override hashCode() when we override equals()?','为什么重写equals方法是必须要重写hashcode','Sure — here is a strong interview-style English answer:\r\n\r\nWhen we override equals(), we should also override hashCode() because Java defines a contract between them: if two objects are equal according to equals(), they must return the same hashCode().\r\n\r\nThis is especially important for hash-based collections such as HashMap, HashSet, and Hashtable. These collections do not use equals() alone. They first use hashCode() to determine which bucket an object belongs to, and then use equals() to compare objects within that bucket.\r\n\r\nIf we override equals() but do not override hashCode(), two objects may be logically equal but still have different hash codes. In that case, they may be placed into different buckets, which can cause incorrect behavior such as:\r\n\r\nduplicate elements appearing in a HashSet\r\ncontains() returning false for an equal object\r\nHashMap.get() failing to find a value for an equal key\r\n\r\nSo, overriding equals() changes the definition of logical equality, and overriding hashCode() ensures that hash-based collections can work correctly with that definition.\r\n\r\nA concise way to say it in an interview is:\r\n\r\nequals() defines whether two objects are logically equal, and hashCode() ensures that equal objects are treated consistently in hash-based collections. That is why they must be overridden together.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 08:08:47',1,'2026-04-16 08:08:47'),(15,7,'5. What is the difference between overloading and overriding?','方法重载和重写的区别？','Overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists in the same class.\r\n重载（Overloading）是指在同一个类中定义多个同名但参数列表不同的方法。\r\n\r\nOverriding means that a subclass provides its own implementation of a method already defined in its superclass.\r\n重写（Overriding）是指子类对父类中已经定义的方法提供自己的实现。','Overloading and overriding are both related to polymorphism in Java, but they are fundamentally different concepts.\r\n重载和重写都与 Java 中的多态有关，但它们本质上是两个不同的概念。\r\n\r\nOverloading means defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists, usually within the same class.\r\n重载是指在同一个类中定义多个同名但参数列表不同的方法。\r\n\r\nIt is an example of compile-time polymorphism because the compiler decides which method to call based on the method signature.\r\n它是编译时多态的一个例子，因为编译器会根据方法签名决定调用哪个方法。\r\n\r\nOverriding means that a subclass provides a new implementation for a method already defined in its superclass.\r\n重写是指子类为父类中已经定义的方法提供新的实现。\r\n\r\nIt is an example of runtime polymorphism because the JVM chooses the actual method implementation based on the real object type at runtime.\r\n它是运行时多态的一个例子，因为 JVM 会在运行时根据对象的真实类型选择实际执行的方法实现。\r\n\r\nIn overloading, the method name stays the same, but the parameter list must be different.\r\n在重载中，方法名保持相同，但参数列表必须不同。\r\n\r\nIn overriding, both the method name and parameter list must be the same as the parent method.\r\n在重写中，方法名和参数列表都必须和父类方法保持一致。\r\n\r\nAlso, overloaded methods can differ in return type, but return type alone cannot distinguish overloaded methods.\r\n另外，重载方法可以有不同的返回值类型，但不能仅靠返回值类型来区分重载方法。\r\n\r\nFor overriding, the return type must be the same or a covariant subtype.\r\n对于重写来说，返回值类型必须相同，或者是协变子类型。\r\n\r\nAnother important difference is that overriding is subject to inheritance rules, such as not reducing access visibility and not throwing broader checked exceptions.\r\n另一个重要区别是，重写必须遵守继承规则，比如不能缩小访问权限，也不能抛出更宽泛的受检异常。\r\n\r\nIn practical development, overloading is used to improve API flexibility and readability, while overriding is used to customize inherited behavior.\r\n在实际开发中，重载通常用于提高 API 的灵活性和可读性，而重写则用于定制继承来的行为。\r\n\r\nIn short, overloading is about same method name with different inputs, while overriding is about redefining inherited behavior in a subclass.\r\n总结来说，重载是同一个方法名处理不同输入，而重写是子类重新定义继承来的行为。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 08:22:51',1,'2026-04-16 08:22:51'),(16,7,'6. What is the difference between final, finally, and finalize()?','final、finally和finalize的区别','final, finally, and finalize() are three completely different concepts in Java, even though their names look similar.\r\nfinal、finally 和 finalize() 在 Java 中是三个完全不同的概念，虽然它们的名字看起来很相似。\r\n\r\nfinal is a keyword used to restrict modification.\r\nfinal 是一个关键字，用来限制修改。\r\n\r\nfinally is a block used in exception handling, and it usually executes whether an exception occurs or not.\r\nfinally 是异常处理中的一个代码块，通常无论是否发生异常都会执行。\r\n\r\nfinalize() is a method defined in the Object class that was originally intended to be called before garbage collection, but it is now deprecated and should not be relied on.\r\nfinalize() 是 Object 类中的一个方法，原本设计目的是在垃圾回收前调用，但现在已经被废弃，不应该再依赖它。\r\n\r\nSo in short, final is about restriction, finally is about cleanup in exception handling, and finalize() is an old garbage collection hook.\r\n所以简单来说，final 关注的是“限制”，finally 关注的是“异常处理中的清理”，而 finalize() 是一个过时的垃圾回收钩子。\r\n\r\nIn modern Java, finalize() is considered unsafe and unreliable, so developers should prefer try-with-resources or explicit cleanup instead.\r\n在现代 Java 中，finalize() 被认为是不安全且不可靠的，因此开发者应该优先使用 try-with-resources 或显式清理资源的方式。','finalize() is a method defined in the Object class.\r\nfinalize() 是定义在 Object 类中的一个方法。\r\n\r\nIts original purpose was to allow an object to perform cleanup before being garbage collected.\r\n它最初的设计目的是让对象在被垃圾回收前有机会做一些清理工作。\r\n\r\nHowever, finalize() has serious problems.\r\n但是，finalize() 有很多严重问题。\r\n\r\nIt is unpredictable because you never know when, or even whether, it will be called.\r\n它是不可预测的，因为你永远不能确定它什么时候会被调用，甚至不能确定它是否一定会被调用。\r\n\r\nIt can also cause performance issues and make object lifecycle harder to understand.\r\n它还可能导致性能问题，并让对象生命周期变得更难理解。\r\n\r\nBecause of these problems, finalize() has been deprecated and is no longer recommended.\r\n正因为这些问题，finalize() 已经被废弃，不再推荐使用。\r\n\r\nIn modern Java, we should use explicit resource management, try-with-resources, or cleaners if needed.\r\n在现代 Java 中，我们应该使用显式资源管理、try-with-resources，或者在必要时使用 cleaner 机制。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 09:31:36',1,'2026-04-16 09:31:36'),(17,7,'7. Why is String immutable in Java?','java中String类型为什么是不可变的','String is immutable in Java for four main reasons: security, thread safety, performance through string pooling, and stable hash codes. Because its value cannot change, it is safe to share, efficient to cache, and reliable to use as a key in hash-based collections.','In Java, String is immutable, which means once a String object is created, its value cannot be changed. If we modify a string, Java actually creates a new object instead of changing the original one.\r\n\r\nThere are several important reasons for this design.\r\n\r\nFirst, security. Strings are widely used in sensitive places such as file paths, URLs, class loading, database connections, and network addresses. If strings were mutable, one part of the code could change the value after validation, which would create security risks.\r\n\r\nSecond, thread safety. Because a string cannot be changed after creation, the same String object can be safely shared across multiple threads without synchronization.\r\n\r\nThird, string pool optimization. Java uses a string pool to reuse string literals and save memory. This only works safely because strings are immutable. If one shared string could be changed, it would affect all references to it.\r\n\r\nFourth, hash code caching. Strings are frequently used as keys in HashMap and similar collections. Since the value never changes, the hash code also stays stable and can be cached, which improves performance.\r\n\r\nFinally, immutability makes strings easier to understand and use. It avoids unexpected side effects and makes code more reliable.\r\n\r\nSo in short, String is immutable because it improves security, enables pooling, supports thread safety, keeps hash-based collections correct, and makes the language more robust.','Java 中 String 不可变，主要原因有：\r\n• 安全性：避免字符串在校验后被篡改\r\n• 线程安全：多个线程可以安全共享同一个字符串对象\r\n• 性能优化：支持字符串常量池复用\r\n• 哈希稳定：适合作为 HashMap 的 key\r\n• 代码可靠性更高：避免副作用','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 09:41:23',1,'2026-04-16 09:41:23'),(18,7,'8. What is the difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap?','HashMap 和 ConcurrentHashMap的区别','The main difference is that HashMap is not thread-safe, while ConcurrentHashMap is designed for thread-safe access in concurrent environments.\r\n\r\nIf multiple threads access and modify a HashMap at the same time, it may lead to race conditions, inconsistent data, or other unpredictable behavior. That is why HashMap is generally used in single-threaded scenarios, or when external synchronization is provided.\r\n\r\nConcurrentHashMap, on the other hand, is built for multithreaded use. It allows multiple threads to read and update the map safely, with much better performance than simply synchronizing the entire map.\r\n\r\nAnother important difference is that HashMap allows one null key and multiple null values, while ConcurrentHashMap does not allow null keys or null values. This is mainly to avoid ambiguity in concurrent operations.\r\n\r\nAlso, the iterator of HashMap is fail-fast, which means it may throw ConcurrentModificationException if the map is modified during iteration. In contrast, ConcurrentHashMap provides weakly consistent iterators, which do not throw that exception and can tolerate concurrent modifications.\r\n\r\nSo in short, HashMap is suitable for non-concurrent use, while ConcurrentHashMap is a better choice when thread safety and concurrent performance are required.','HashMap 和 ConcurrentHashMap 最主要的区别是：HashMap 不是线程安全的，而 ConcurrentHashMap 是为并发环境设计的线程安全 Map。\r\n\r\n如果多个线程同时访问并修改 HashMap，就可能出现竞态条件、数据不一致，或者其他不可预测的问题。所以 HashMap 一般用于单线程场景，或者由外部代码自己做同步控制。\r\n\r\n而 ConcurrentHashMap 是专门为多线程场景设计的。它允许多个线程安全地同时读取和更新数据，并且相比简单地把整个 Map 同步起来，它的并发性能更好。\r\n\r\n另一个重要区别是，HashMap 允许一个 null 键和多个 null 值，而 ConcurrentHashMap 不允许 null 键和 null 值。这主要是为了避免并发操作时产生歧义。\r\n\r\n此外，HashMap 的迭代器是 fail-fast 的，也就是说在遍历过程中如果 Map 被结构性修改，可能会抛出 ConcurrentModificationException。而 ConcurrentHashMap 的迭代器是弱一致性的，不会抛出这个异常，并且能够容忍并发修改。\r\n\r\n所以简单来说，HashMap 更适合非并发场景，而 ConcurrentHashMap 更适合需要线程安全和并发性能的场景。','全中文解释\r\n\r\n这道题最核心就抓住三点：\r\n\r\n1. 线程安全不同\r\n\r\nHashMap 不是线程安全的。\r\n如果多个线程同时去改它，可能出现问题。\r\n\r\nConcurrentHashMap 是线程安全的。\r\n它就是专门为多线程场景准备的。\r\n\r\n2. 并发性能不同\r\nConcurrentHashMap 内部做了更细粒度的并发控制，所以在高并发下性能通常更好\r\n\r\n3. 对 null 的支持不同\r\n\r\nHashMap：\r\n• 允许一个 null key\r\n• 允许多个 null value\r\n\r\nConcurrentHashMap：\r\n• 不允许 null key\r\n• 不允许 null value\r\n\r\n为什么不允许？\r\n因为在并发环境里，如果 get(key) 返回 null，就没法立刻判断：\r\n• 是这个 key 根本不存在\r\n• 还是这个 key 的 value 就是 null\r\n\r\n为了避免这种歧义，ConcurrentHashMap 直接禁止 null。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 09:51:16',1,'2026-04-16 09:51:16'),(19,7,'9. How does HashMap work internally?','HashMap的内部工作原理？','Internally, HashMap stores data in the form of key-value pairs, and it uses an array plus a hashing mechanism to locate where each entry should be stored.\r\n\r\nWhen we put a key-value pair into a HashMap, Java first calculates the hash code of the key by calling hashCode(). Then it applies an additional hash operation to reduce collisions and determines the index in the internal bucket array.\r\n\r\nIf the target bucket is empty, the new entry is stored directly there.\r\nIf the bucket already contains entries, a collision happens. In that case, HashMap uses the equals() method to compare keys and decide whether it should replace an existing value or add a new entry.\r\n\r\nIn older implementations, collided entries were stored in a linked list. In Java 8 and later, if too many entries are stored in the same bucket and the bucket becomes large enough, the linked list can be converted into a red-black tree to improve lookup performance.\r\n\r\nWhen we call get(), HashMap again computes the hash of the key, finds the correct bucket, and then checks the entries in that bucket using equals() until it finds the matching key.\r\n\r\nSo internally, HashMap mainly relies on:\r\n	•	hashCode() to find the bucket\r\n	•	equals() to find the correct key inside the bucket\r\n	•	an array as the main storage structure\r\n	•	linked lists or red-black trees to handle collisions','','HashMap 内部是以键值对的形式存储数据的，它主要依赖 数组 + 哈希算法 来决定每个元素应该放在哪个位置。\r\n\r\n当我们往 HashMap 中放入一个键值对时，Java 会先通过 key 的 hashCode() 方法计算出哈希值。然后再经过一次额外的扰动处理，来减少哈希冲突，并最终计算出它在底层数组中的桶位置。\r\n\r\n如果这个桶是空的，那么这个元素就会直接放进去。\r\n如果这个桶里已经有元素了，就说明发生了哈希冲突。这时候 HashMap 会继续使用 equals() 方法比较 key，判断是替换已有值，还是把新的节点追加进去。\r\n\r\n在早期实现中，发生冲突的节点会以链表的形式存储在同一个桶里。\r\n从 Java 8 开始，如果某个桶里的元素太多，并且达到一定条件，链表会转换成红黑树，这样可以提升查找效率。\r\n\r\n当我们调用 get() 时，HashMap 会再次对 key 计算哈希值，找到对应的桶，然后在桶中通过 equals() 比较，直到找到目标 key。\r\n\r\n所以从底层原理来说，HashMap 主要依赖：\r\n	•	hashCode() 来定位桶\r\n	•	equals() 来定位桶中的具体 key\r\n	•	数组作为主要存储结构\r\n	•	链表或红黑树来解决哈希冲突','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 10:11:59',1,'2026-04-16 10:11:59'),(20,7,'10. Under what conditions will a linked list in a HashMap be converted into a red-black tree?','什情况下HashMap中的链表会转换成红黑树 ？','When the number of nodes in a single bucket reaches 8, HashMap will consider converting the linked list into a red-black tree. However, this conversion does not happen immediately in every case.\r\n\r\nThere are two important conditions:\r\n\r\nFirst, the bucket size must reach the treeify threshold, which is 8.\r\nSecond, the overall table capacity must be at least 64.\r\n\r\nSo, in Java 8 and later, a linked list is converted into a red-black tree only when:\r\n	•	the number of nodes in the bucket is 8 or more\r\n	•	and the table capacity is at least 64\r\n\r\nIf the bucket size reaches 8 but the table capacity is still less than 64, HashMap will resize the table first instead of treeifying the bucket.\r\n\r\nThe reason is that if the table is still small, too many collisions may simply mean the table needs more space. After resizing, entries may be redistributed into different buckets, so the long linked list may disappear naturally. This is usually more efficient than converting it into a red-black tree too early.\r\n\r\nThere is also an untreeify threshold, which is 6. That means if the number of nodes in a tree-based bucket later drops below 6, it may be converted back into a linked list. This helps avoid frequent switching between linked lists and trees when the number of elements changes around the boundary.\r\n\r\nSo the key thresholds are:\r\n	•	8 for treeification\r\n	•	6 for untreeification\r\n	•	64 as the minimum table capacity for treeification\r\n\r\nIn short, in Java 8+, a bucket is considered for treeification when its size reaches 8, but it will only actually become a red-black tree if the table capacity is at least 64. Otherwise, HashMap resizes first.','','在 Java 8 里，当某个 bucket 的节点数达到 8 时，HashMap 会考虑把链表转成红黑树，但前提是整个 table 的容量至少要达到 64。如果容量还不到 64，就会先扩容而不是树化。后续如果树中的节点数又降到 6 以下，在某些情况下还会退化回链表。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 10:16:34',1,'2026-04-16 10:16:34'),(21,7,'11. What is the difference between HashSet and TreeSet?','HashSet 和 TreeSet 的区别','The main difference between HashSet and TreeSet is that HashSet stores elements in no particular order, while TreeSet stores elements in sorted order.\r\n\r\nHashSet is implemented based on a hash table, so it provides very fast performance for basic operations such as add, remove, and contains. In general, these operations are close to O(1) on average.\r\n\r\nTreeSet, on the other hand, is implemented based on a red-black tree. It keeps elements sorted either by their natural ordering or by a custom Comparator. Because it uses a tree structure, operations like add, remove, and contains usually take O(log n) time.\r\n\r\nAnother difference is that HashSet allows one null element, while TreeSet usually does not allow null, especially when natural ordering is used, because null cannot be compared with other elements.\r\n\r\nIn terms of usage, HashSet is better when you only care about uniqueness and performance, while TreeSet is better when you need both uniqueness and sorted order.\r\n\r\nSo in short, HashSet is faster and unordered, while TreeSet is ordered but usually slower because it maintains sorting','HashSet 和 TreeSet 最主要的区别是：HashSet 中的元素是无序的，而 TreeSet 中的元素是有序的。\r\n\r\nHashSet 底层基于哈希表实现，所以在 add、remove、contains 这类基本操作上通常性能很高，平均时间复杂度接近 O(1)。\r\n\r\n而 TreeSet 底层基于红黑树实现，它会按照元素的自然顺序，或者你提供的 Comparator 来对元素进行排序。因为使用的是树结构，所以 add、remove、contains 的时间复杂度通常是 O(log n)。\r\n\r\n另一个区别是，HashSet 允许一个 null 元素，而 TreeSet 通常不允许 null，特别是在使用自然排序的时候，因为 null 不能和其他元素进行比较。\r\n\r\n从使用场景来看，如果你只关心去重和性能，那么 HashSet 更合适；如果你不仅要去重，还要让元素始终保持排序，那么 TreeSet 更合适。\r\n\r\n所以简单来说，HashSet 更快但无序，而 TreeSet 有序但因为要维护排序，通常会慢一些。','1. 底层结构不同\r\nHashSet 的底层是：\r\n👉 HashMap\r\n它本质上是用 HashMap 来保存元素的，只不过只用 key，不太关心 value。\r\n\r\n而 TreeSet 的底层是：\r\n👉 红黑树（实际上通常基于 TreeMap）\r\n\r\n所以它天然有排序能力。\r\n\r\n2. 顺序不同\r\nHashSet  不保证插入顺序 /  不保证排序顺序  / 看起来就是“无序”\r\nTreeSet 	元素会自动排序 / 默认按自然顺序排  /   也可以传 Comparator 自定义排序规则\r\n\r\n\r\n3. 性能不同\r\nHashSet\r\n因为是哈希表，所以：add  remove contains 平均复杂度通常是  👉 O(1)\r\n\r\nTreeSet\r\n\r\n因为是红黑树，所以这些操作通常是 👉 O(log n)\r\n所以一般来说：👉 HashSet 更快  👉 TreeSet 更适合有序场景\r\n\r\n4. 对 null 的支持不同\r\n\r\nHashSet 允许一个 null\r\nTreeSet 通常不允许 null，因为 TreeSet 需要比较大小，而 null 通常没法比较，所以会报错。\r\n\r\n5. 什么时候用哪个\r\n\r\n用 HashSet 如果你只是想：去重  / 	查得快  / 	不关心顺序  /  那用 HashSet\r\n\r\n用 TreeSet 如果你想： 	去重  / 自动排序  /  还可能要取最小值、最大值、范围查找 那用 TreeSet','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-16 14:19:46',1,'2026-04-16 14:19:46'),(22,2,'Tell me about yourself.','请介绍一下你自己。','Hi, I’m Shuang Wu. I’m a senior software engineer with over ten years of experience in backend development and distributed systems. I’ve worked in industries such as finance, e-commerce, telecommunications, and new energy vehicles, building reliable high-traffic systems. I recently moved to New Zealand and am studying Applied Computing at Lincoln University. I’m now looking for opportunities to contribute my backend and system design experience to the local tech industry.','Hi, I’m Shuang Wu. I’m a senior software engineer with over ten years of hands-on experience in backend development, system architecture, and large-scale distributed systems. I’ve worked across multiple industries including finance, e-commerce, new energy vehicles, and telecommunications, and I’ve delivered end-to-end solutions for high-traffic, business-critical systems.\r\n\r\nIn my previous roles, I was often the lead engineer responsible for complex system design, stability optimisation, and cross-team collaboration. I’m very comfortable taking ownership of difficult tasks, breaking down complicated problems, and delivering reliable solutions under tight deadlines.\r\n\r\nI recently moved to New Zealand and am continuing m y studies in Applied Computing at Lincoln University, with the goal of integrating into the local tech industry and contributing my experience in backend engineering, microservices, and cloud-native systems. I bring strong work discipline, fast learning ability, and a calm, practical approach to solving problems.','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 11:53:11',1,'2026-04-20 11:53:11'),(23,2,'What are your strengths?','你的优势是什么？','I would say my main strengths are problem-solving, ownership, and adaptability. Over the years, I’ve worked on backend development, distributed systems, and system optimisation, so I’ve had a lot of experience dealing with complex technical issues. When problems come up, I usually stay calm, analyse the root cause, and focus on finding a practical and reliable solution. Another strength is that I take responsibility for my work. If I’m involved in a task or project, I like to follow it through and make sure it is completed properly. I also adapt quickly to new environments and new business domains, which has helped me work across different industries like finance, e-commerce, telecommunications, and new energy vehicles.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 17:45:33',1,'2026-04-20 17:45:33'),(24,2,'What are your weaknesses?','你的弱势是什么？','One weakness I’ve noticed in myself is that I can sometimes be too focused on details, especially when I want the final result to be stable and high quality. In software engineering, that can be a strength, but it can also slow me down if I spend too much time polishing less important parts. Over time, I’ve become more aware of this, and I’ve been working on balancing quality with efficiency. Now I try to prioritise tasks more clearly, focus on the parts that create the most value, and communicate earlier with teammates when a trade-off is needed. I still care a lot about quality, but I’ve learned to manage my time better and work more strategically.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 17:50:01',1,'2026-04-20 17:50:01'),(25,2,'Where do you see yourself in 5 years?','你未来五年的职业规划','In five years, I see myself continuing to grow as a strong backend engineer or technical lead here in New Zealand. I’d like to be in a role where I can contribute not only through coding, but also through system design, technical decision-making, and improving system reliability and scalability. I also hope to deepen my knowledge in cloud-native systems and modern software architecture. At the same time, I want to become someone the team can rely on, not only for technical delivery, but also for collaboration, mentoring junior engineers, and helping projects move forward smoothly. Overall, I hope to build a stable long-term career where I can keep learning and make meaningful contributions to the company.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 17:50:42',1,'2026-04-20 17:50:42'),(26,2,'What motivates you?','什么激励你？','What motivates me most is solving meaningful problems and seeing the real impact of my work. As a software engineer, I enjoy building systems that are not only technically sound, but also useful for the business and for users. I feel motivated when I can take a complex problem, break it down, and turn it into a reliable solution. I’m also motivated by continuous learning. Technology changes quickly, and I enjoy improving my skills, whether that means learning new tools, understanding a new business domain, or becoming better at system design. In addition, I’m motivated by working with a professional team where people support each other and work toward a common goal','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 17:55:18',1,'2026-04-20 17:55:18'),(27,2,'How do you handle stress and pressure?','你如何应对压力？','I usually handle stress and pressure by staying calm, focusing on priorities, and approaching the problem step by step. In software engineering, pressure often comes from deadlines, production issues, or unexpected technical problems, so I’ve learned that panic does not help. Instead, I first try to understand the situation clearly, identify the most critical issue, and deal with the highest-priority task first. I also believe communication is very important under pressure. If I’m working with a team, I make sure everyone is aligned on what needs to be done and what the risks are. In general, I see pressure as part of the job, and I try to turn it into structured action rather than emotional stress.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-20 17:57:07',1,'2026-04-20 17:57:07'),(28,2,'1. Give me an example of a time when you have needed to use technology\r\nto solve a problem.\r\nWhat was the technology and how did you use it?\r\nIn what ways did it add value?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','1.举例说明你用技术来解决一个问题？你用的什么技术？怎么用的？它是怎么实现价值的？收益是什么？','In my previous company, we needed to refactor a product system and redesign part of the original database structure. One important requirement was that the business could not be interrupted during the migration.\r\n\r\nTo reduce the risk, we needed to keep both the old and new systems running at the same time. So whenever a new record was inserted into the new system, we also needed to make sure the corresponding data was still available in the old system. This allowed us to switch back to the old system quickly if any issue happened.\r\n\r\nTo solve this problem, I researched several data synchronisation middleware options, including Canal, Debezium, and Flink CDC. I compared them from different perspectives, such as implementation complexity, ease of use, maintenance cost, and how well they fitted our system. In the end, we chose Debezium.\r\n\r\nIn the project, I introduced Debezium, set up a Kafka cluster, and enabled MySQL binlog to support both historical data synchronisation and incremental data synchronisation. This allowed us to keep data consistent between the old and new systems during the migration.\r\n\r\nThe main benefit was that we achieved a smooth system migration without interrupting the business. At the same time, the new product system became more flexible and could support more configurable product rules. After the project was completed, we also promoted this technical solution within the department, and it became a standard approach for similar system migration projects.','','在我的上一家公司，我们需要对一个产品系统进行重构，改造原来的数据库的一些结构，我们需要保证在业务不中断的情况下，完成这个系统改造。为了保证有问题随时切换新旧系统，我们需要在新系统插入一条数据的同时，在旧系统还要插插入一条数据。处理这个问题，我在调研了几种同步的中间件，有canal，debezium，和Flink CDC，从复杂度、易用性和使用成本上考虑，最终确定了debezium。然后在项目中引入debezium ,搭建kafka集群，开启mysql的binlog实现数据的存量和增量同步，真个技术实施的收益是我们做到了不中断业务实现系统的平滑迁移，同时使原有的产品系统支持更灵活的产品配置，同时在部门内进行技术方案的推广，成为系统迁移的一个标准方案','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 07:32:46',1,'2026-04-25 07:32:46'),(30,2,'2. Give me an example of a time when you needed to use market intel to\r\ngather information about technology or a digital tool.\r\nWhat did you do to get further information?\r\nWhat were your objectives?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','请举一个你调研技术或数字工具的例子。你是如何收集信息、进一步了解这个工具的？你的目标是什么？最后取得了什么结果？','In one of my previous roles, I worked on a small advertising system. One of the requirements was to implement ad rotation based on ad impressions. We also needed to collect users’ clicks and views in real time. Once an ad placement reached a certain impression threshold, the system needed to rotate to the next ad.\r\n\r\nAt that time, I had some basic knowledge of Flink. I knew it was a stream processing framework, but I had not used it in a real project before. So I started by reading the official Apache Flink documentation to understand the basic workflow and core concepts.\r\n\r\nAfter that, I looked for more practical information from technical communities, such as Stack Overflow, the Apache Flink community, Confluent articles, and Kafka documentation. Since AI tools were not available at that time, I mainly relied on official documentation, community discussions, and technical articles.\r\n\r\nMy objective was to understand Flink’s capabilities and implementation details, such as streams, windows, tumbling windows, sliding windows, and watermarks, and then apply them to our business scenario.\r\n\r\nIn the end, I successfully delivered the advertising system and put it into production. The system ran stably, and later I also shared my research process and some practical Flink experience with the team during our weekly technical review meeting.','','之前我在工作做一个小型的广告系统的时候，需要实现广告位的曝光轮换，需要对用户的点击和浏览进行实时统计，如果一个广告位达到曝光度就轮换下一个广告位。因为之前对Flink有一点了解，知道它就是流式计算框架，但是没有真正使用过，所以我会首先找到官方的网站通读一遍使用文档，理解了它的一个大概使用流程，然后会结合一些技术社区的文章如Stack Overflow，Apache Flink Community、Confluent 和 Kafka Documentation等，因为当时还没有AI出现，所以不能借助AI的能力，我的目标就是深入了解它的能力，和使用细节，如Stream，window， Tumbling Window，Watermark等，最终实现我们的业务。我最终将整个广告能力系统上线，并且运行稳定，后来在技术总结周会中也和大家分享了我的调用过程和flink使用的一些经验','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 08:29:08',1,'2026-04-25 08:29:08'),(31,2,'4. Give me an example of where you shared knowledge and expertise\r\nrelative to technology with others.\r\nWhat was the situation?\r\nHow important was the sharing of your knowledge and expertise?','3.举一个你和其他人分享技术相关的知识和专业的例子\r\n说明当时的情况\r\n你分享专业知识有多重要？','During one production incident, we had just released some new features, and one function became very slow in the production environment.\r\n\r\nAfter checking the MySQL logs, we found that one SQL query was not using the expected index properly. One of my colleagues then directly dropped the existing index and recreated a new one. However, because he dropped the index first, other queries could no longer use it and started doing full table scans. As a result, the whole system became unavailable, and the business was affected for about half an hour before the database finally recovered.\r\n\r\nAfter this incident, I used it as a case study and shared it with the team. I explained how to troubleshoot slow system performance, including checking logs, identifying slow SQL queries, and analysing index usage.\r\n\r\nThe key lesson I emphasised was that when changing indexes in production, we should add the new index first and only remove the old one after confirming the new index works correctly. Otherwise, a small optimisation may cause a much bigger production issue.','','再一次处理生产问题的时候，由于上了一些新功能，生产环境中出现了某一个功能相应很慢的问题，通过查询mysql的日志，发现是某一条sql语句没有命中全部索引，所以我的一个同事就直接删掉索引并重新建立一个索引，但是由于他先删除了索引，导致其他查询直接全表扫描，真个系统直接瘫痪了，最后导致业务停滞了半小时，真个库才恢复正常，我就把此次的经验作为一个案例分享，和大家分享了如何排查系统运行缓慢地问题，然后着重强调了更改索引的时候一定要先添加索引再删除索引，否者会引起更大的灾难','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 08:51:38',1,'2026-04-25 08:51:38'),(32,2,'5. When have you needed to use / understand customer data to inform a\r\ndecision?\r\nWhat specifically do you do?\r\nWhen has it been especially important to make sure that you do this?','请举一个例子，说明你曾经如何通过分析客户数据来帮助你做决定。你具体分析了哪些数据、采取了什么行动？在什么情况下，确保基于客户数据做决策特别重要？','In one of my previous companies, I worked on an online used-car e-commerce platform. At that time, we had a marketing campaign, and the business team wanted to run an A/B test before launching the final campaign page to all users.\r\n\r\nWe created two versions of the page: Page A and Page B. Some users were directed to Page A, and others were directed to Page B. Then we collected user click data and compared the click-through rate of each page.\r\n\r\nMy role was to implement the tracking points for collecting user behaviour data, process the click data, and use visualisation tools to show the page performance statistics clearly.\r\n\r\nBased on the data, the business team could decide which page performed better and then release the better version to all users.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was that we used customer data to support a business decision, reduced the risk of launching the wrong page, and achieved better results with lower cost.','','这个例子是我几年前的一家公司，我门是负责一家二手车的网上电商网站，有一个活动需要做AB测试，我们做了两个页面，投放了一部分用户，一些用户访问Page A 一部分访问Page B ,然后收集点击数据，然后分析每个页面的点击率，最终决定用哪一个页面作为最终的活动页面然后在放开全量。我在这个过程实现数据埋点的收集和计算，利用可视化的图标工具来展示页面的统计工具，我所做的工作给业务决策提供了数据依据，以最小的成本获取最大的收益','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 15:20:23',1,'2026-04-25 15:20:23'),(33,2,'6. Give me an example of a time when something was unclear and you\r\nhave needed to probe information and assumptions in order to clarify a\r\nsituation.\r\nWhat was the information?\r\nWhat was the situation?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','6. 请举一个例子，说明当某件事情不清楚时，你需要进一步追问信息和验证假设，从而澄清情况。\r\n当时的信息是什么？\r\n当时是什么情况？\r\n最终结果是什么？\r\n\r\n更自然的中文理解是：\r\n\r\n请讲一个你遇到信息不明确、情况不清楚的例子。你是如何通过追问、调查、验证假设，把问题弄清楚的？当时你掌握了哪些信息？具体发生了什么？最后结果怎么样？','In my previous company, I worked on a membership product system migration project. At the beginning, the business requirements were not very clear. The business team mainly said that the old system was difficult to use, product configuration was becoming more complex, and they wanted the new system to support membership packages and entitlement rules more flexibly without interrupting the existing business.\r\n\r\nThe unclear part was how we should keep the old and new systems running at the same time. Some people assumed that we could simply write data into both systems in the application code. But I was not sure whether this would be safe, because there were many existing product records, and we also had to handle new records created during the migration period.\r\n\r\nSo I started to probe the available information and assumptions. I asked the product owner and business team which product information was business-critical, which data would directly affect membership benefits or customer experience, and whether the business could tolerate any interruption during the migration. I also checked the current database structure, data volume, and write frequency. From the technical side, we then identified the critical fields, considered the rollback plan, and discussed whether dual writing in the application layer would bring risks, such as data inconsistency or higher maintenance cost.\r\n\r\nBased on this investigation, I found that we needed both historical data synchronisation and incremental data synchronisation. So I researched several CDC solutions, including Canal, Debezium, and Flink CDC. After comparing complexity, ease of use, and maintenance cost, we decided to use Debezium with Kafka and MySQL binlog.\r\n\r\nAs a result, we clarified the real requirement and avoided making a risky assumption. We successfully kept the old and new systems in sync during the migration, and the business was not interrupted. The solution also became a reusable approach for similar system migration projects in our department.','','在我上一家公司，我参与了一个会员产品系统迁移项目。项目一开始需求并不是特别清楚。业务方只是说，他们想重新设计一部分产品数据库结构，并且在不中断业务的情况下迁移到新系统。\r\n\r\n当时不清楚的地方是：我们应该如何让新旧系统同时运行。有些人认为可以直接在应用代码里双写，也就是同时往新旧两个系统写数据。但我不确定这样是否安全，因为系统里已经有很多历史产品数据，同时在迁移期间还会不断产生新数据。\r\n\r\n所以我开始深入核实已有信息和假设。我向产品负责人和业务团队确认哪些产品信息在业务上最关键，哪些数据会直接影响会员权益或客户体验，以及迁移期间业务是否能够接受中断。同时，我检查了当前数据库结构、数据量和写入频率。然后从技术角度，我们识别出关键字段，考虑回滚方案，并讨论在应用层做双写是否会带来数据不一致或维护成本更高的风险。\r\n\r\n通过调查，我发现我们实际上需要同时解决两个问题：历史数据同步和增量数据同步。所以我调研了几种 CDC 方案，包括 Canal、Debezium 和 Flink CDC。最后我们从复杂度、易用性和维护成本考虑，选择了 Debezium，结合 Kafka 和 MySQL binlog 实现同步。\r\n\r\n最终结果是，我们澄清了真实需求，避免了直接基于错误假设做方案。我们成功实现了新旧系统在迁移期间的数据同步，业务没有中断。这个方案后来也成为部门里类似系统迁移项目的可复用方案。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 17:44:25',1,'2026-04-28 17:24:23'),(34,1,'7. When have you needed to comply with processes/governance\r\nrequirements in your role?\r\nExplain the situation.\r\nHow did you react?\r\nHow did the process relate to your role?','7. 在你的工作角色中，你什么时候需要遵守流程或治理要求？\r\n请解释当时的情况。\r\n你是如何应对的？\r\n这个流程和你的工作职责有什么关系？\r\n\r\n更自然的中文理解是：\r\n\r\n请举一个例子，说明你在工作中需要遵守公司流程、审批制度、合规要求或技术治理规范的经历。当时发生了什么？你是怎么处理的？这个流程为什么和你的岗位职责有关？','In my last role, I worked for a telecommunications company. The company had very strict release approval processes, especially during major national events, because we had to make sure our systems were stable and reliable.\r\n\r\nFor any production release, we had to submit a series of approval forms. These forms needed to include the code review results, testing results, and other release-related information. The release could only go ahead after approval from the next-level manager.\r\n\r\nBefore the code moved into the testing stage, we also had a two-person code review process. If the code did not pass the review, it could not be merged. After testing, the QA team had to provide a final test report before the release could be approved.\r\n\r\nAs a Senior Engineer, my role was not only to complete my own development work, but also to mentor junior and mid-level engineers. I was responsible for reviewing technical design, controlling code quality, and helping reduce production risks. If a production issue was caused by work from a junior engineer I supported, I also shared responsibility for that issue.\r\n\r\nSo I took these governance requirements seriously. I saw them as part of my role to ensure system stability, improve delivery quality, and protect the business from unnecessary production risks.','','我的最后一份工作是在电信公司，我们公司有着非常严格的上线审批规范，尤其在有国家的重大活动时，我们需要保证我们的系统稳定运行，我们有着严格的审批流程，上线系统需要提交一些列的表单，必须要附加代码review的结果，测试结果等，且需要上一级领导的审批后才可以上线，我门在对代码测试前进行双人的代码review，不能通过的代码不能进行合并，测试团队必须给出最终的测试报告，我的title是senior工程师，平时负责辅导一些初中级的工程师，负责把控技术设计和代码质量，对于一些初级工程师的生产问题负有一定责任。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 17:56:51',1,'2026-04-25 17:56:51'),(35,2,'8. Give me an example of when you have explained data-based\r\ninformation and concepts to others.\r\nWhat techniques did you use for explaining?\r\nHow was it received?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','8. 请举一个例子，说明你曾经向他人解释基于数据的信息或概念。\r\n你用了什么方法来解释？\r\n别人是如何接受或反馈的？\r\n最终结果是什么？','In my previous role, I had to explain system performance data to both technical team members and non-technical stakeholders after a production issue.\r\n\r\nAt that time, some users reported that the system was slow. To understand the real situation, I checked three key metrics: response time, error rate, and system throughput.\r\n\r\nFirst, I explained response time as how long the system takes to respond to a user request. Then I explained error rate as the percentage of failed requests, and throughput as how many requests the system can process in a certain period of time.\r\n\r\nTo make it easier to understand, I did not only show raw numbers. I used a dashboard and simple charts to compare the normal performance with the problematic period. For example, I showed that the average response time had increased from around 300 milliseconds to more than 2 seconds, while the error rate also increased during the same time window. At the same time, the throughput dropped, which meant the system was processing fewer requests than usual.\r\n\r\nI also explained the relationship between these metrics in simple language. A high response time meant users were waiting longer. A high error rate meant more users were experiencing failed requests. Lower throughput meant the system capacity was affected. This helped the team and stakeholders understand that it was not just a small delay, but a real system performance issue.\r\n\r\nThe information was well received because I used visual charts, avoided too much technical jargon, and linked the data directly to user impact and business impact. The team quickly agreed on the priority of the issue.\r\n\r\nAs a result, we identified the slow database queries, optimised the indexes, and improved the API performance. After the fix, the response time returned to the normal level, the error rate dropped, and the system throughput became stable again. This experience showed me that clearly explaining data-based information is very important, especially when different people need to make decisions based on the same data.','','在之前的工作中，生产环境出现系统变慢的问题后，我需要向技术团队和非技术相关人员解释系统性能数据。当时我主要用了三个指标：响应时间、错误率和系统吞吐量。\r\n\r\n我先用简单的话解释这三个概念：响应时间是系统处理用户请求需要多久；错误率是失败请求的比例；吞吐量是系统在一定时间内能处理多少请求。为了让大家更容易理解，我没有只展示原始数字，而是用 dashboard 和图表对比正常时间段和故障时间段的数据。\r\n\r\n例如，我展示了平均响应时间从 300ms 上升到 2 秒以上，同时错误率也升高，而系统吞吐量下降。这说明系统不是单纯“有点慢”，而是真的出现了性能问题。我还把这些数据和用户体验、业务影响联系起来说明。\r\n\r\n最后，团队很快理解了问题的严重性，并优先处理。我们定位到慢 SQL，优化了索引和 API 性能。修复后，响应时间恢复正常，错误率下降，系统吞吐量也稳定了。\r\n\r\n面试时你可以重点强调这句话：\r\n\r\nI used simple language, visual charts, and business impact to explain the technical data clearly.\r\n\r\n意思是：\r\n\r\n我用简单语言、可视化图表和业务影响来清楚解释技术数据。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-25 18:04:15',1,'2026-04-25 18:04:15'),(36,2,'9. Describe a flexible approach which you have taken towards a problem.\r\nWhat was the issue?\r\nIn which way were you flexible?\r\nWhat feedback did you receive?','9. 请描述一次你用灵活的方法处理问题的经历。\r\n\r\n当时的问题是什么？\r\n你在哪些方面表现出了灵活性？\r\n你收到了什么反馈？\r\n\r\n更自然的中文理解是：\r\n\r\n请举一个例子，说明你遇到问题时没有死板地坚持原计划，而是根据实际情况调整了方法。你遇到的是什么问题？你具体做了哪些灵活调整？别人对你的处理方式有什么反馈？','In one of my previous companies, I worked on an e-commerce platform. Many product requirements were temporary experiments. Sometimes a feature would be launched for only a few days, and if the result was not good, it would be removed quickly.\r\n\r\nThe issue was that these requirements were always different, and the timeline was usually very tight. It was not practical to follow the full design and development process for every small temporary feature, because that would create too much development effort, too many database changes, and a higher release risk.\r\n\r\nSo I took a more flexible approach. Instead of hard-coding every experimental feature or creating new database tables each time, we designed a general configuration-based solution. We stored the experiment-related data as JSON, so most small changes could be made by updating the configuration rather than changing the code or database structure.\r\n\r\nThis made the system much more flexible. For many minor changes, the product team only needed to update the JSON configuration, and the change could take effect quickly without a new application release.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was very positive. It met the product team’s need for fast experimentation and rapid iteration, while also reducing the risks caused by frequent releases. It also helped us avoid creating messy temporary tables and one-off code for short-term experiments.\r\n\r\nThe feedback from the product team was very good. They were happy with our quick response, because some page changes could be completed within hours, or even minutes. This configuration-based solution was kept and reused for many later experimental features.','','在我们的电商平台中，有很多需求功能都是临时的实验形式的，经常上线几天如果感觉效果不好就会下掉，每一次的需求都是各种各样的而且时间非常紧急，我门不可能对每一个临时紧急的功能点进行正常的设计和开发，我想到了一个完美的方式，我们开发了一个通用的配置功能，实验的数据以非格式化文本JSON储存，这样减少了数据库的更改，而且每次小的调整仅需修改json数据即可，没有开发工作量，而且无需上线程序，这即满足了产品的实验性的快速迭代需求，也降低了频繁发布所产生的风险，这个功能一直被沿用，产品团队对我们的快速响应非常满意，不但解决了临时实验性的需求所产生的垃圾表结构和代码问题，并且可以小时级别甚至分钟级别的修改页面并生效','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 06:50:52',1,'2026-04-26 06:50:52'),(37,1,'9. Describe a flexible approach which you have taken towards a problem. What was the issue? In which way were you flexible? What feedback did you receive?','9. 请描述一次你用灵活的方法处理问题的经历。\r\n\r\n当时的问题是什么？\r\n你在哪些方面表现出了灵活性？\r\n你收到了什么反馈？\r\n\r\n更自然的中文理解是：\r\n\r\n请举一个例子，说明你遇到问题时没有死板地坚持原计划，而是根据实际情况调整了方法。你遇到的是什么问题？你具体做了哪些灵活调整？别人对你的处理方式有什么反馈？','In one of my previous companies, I worked on an e-commerce platform where many product requirements were temporary experiments. Some features were only launched for a few days, and if the business result was not good, they would be removed quickly.\r\n\r\nThe issue was that these requirements changed frequently, and the timeline was usually very tight. At first, the product team expected us to design and develop each experimental feature in the normal way. However, I realised this would create several risks: too many temporary database tables, duplicated one-off code, frequent releases, and a higher chance of production issues.\r\n\r\nSo instead of refusing the changes or simply following the original development process, I accepted the business need for fast experimentation and suggested a more flexible and lower-risk approach. I proposed a general configuration-based solution. We stored experiment-related data in JSON configuration, so many small changes could be made by updating the configuration rather than changing code or database tables.\r\n\r\nTo reduce risk, I also worked with the team to divide responsibilities clearly. Backend engineers were responsible for designing the configuration structure and validation rules. Frontend engineers were responsible for rendering the page based on the configuration. QA focused on testing different configuration scenarios, and the product owner was responsible for confirming the business rules before the configuration was changed.\r\n\r\nThis approach helped the team work together instead of treating each request as an isolated urgent task. It also allowed us to make straightforward decisions based on the information we had: if a requirement was temporary and experimental, we used configuration; if it became a long-term core feature, we would then consider proper database design and full development.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was very positive. The product team could make page changes within hours, or even minutes, without waiting for a full release. At the same time, we reduced the risks caused by frequent deployments and avoided creating messy temporary tables and throwaway code.\r\n\r\nThe feedback from the product team was very good. They appreciated that we responded quickly to changing business needs while still controlling technical risk. The solution was later reused for many other experimental features.','在我之前的一家公司，我参与了一个电商平台的开发。这个平台经常会有一些临时性的产品实验需求。有些功能可能只上线几天，如果业务效果不好，就会很快下线。\r\n\r\n当时的问题是，这类需求变化非常频繁，而且时间通常很紧。最开始，产品团队希望每一个实验性功能都按照正常的设计和开发流程来做。但是我判断这样会带来几个风险：会产生很多临时数据库表、很多一次性的代码、频繁上线发布，以及更高的生产环境风险。\r\n\r\n所以我没有简单拒绝这些变化，也没有机械地按照原来的开发方式去做，而是接受了业务快速试验的需求，同时提出了一个更灵活、风险更低的方案。我建议开发一个通用的配置化功能，把实验相关的数据用 JSON 配置的方式保存。这样很多小的页面调整或规则变化，只需要修改配置，而不需要频繁改代码、改数据库表或重新发布系统。\r\n\r\n为了降低风险，我也和团队一起明确了分工。后端工程师负责设计配置结构和校验规则，前端工程师负责根据配置动态渲染页面，测试团队重点测试不同配置场景，产品负责人负责在配置变更前确认业务规则。\r\n\r\n这个方案让团队可以作为一个整体协作，而不是每个人单独应对临时紧急需求。我们也根据需求性质做了一个清晰判断：如果是短期实验性需求，就走配置化方案；如果未来变成长期核心功能，再按照正常流程进行数据库设计和完整开发。\r\n\r\n最终结果非常好。产品团队可以在几个小时内，甚至几分钟内完成页面或规则调整，而不需要等待完整的系统发布。同时，我们也降低了频繁上线带来的风险，避免了大量临时表和一次性代码对系统造成长期污染。\r\n\r\n产品团队对这个方案反馈很好，他们认为我们既能快速响应业务变化，又能控制技术风险。后来这个配置化方案也被继续沿用，用在了很多类似的实验性功能中。','你可以特别记住这几句，比较贴合 Risk intelligence 的加分点：\r\n\r\nI accepted the business need for fast experimentation, but I also identified the risks of frequent releases and temporary code.\r\n\r\n我接受了业务快速试验的需求变化，但同时识别出了频繁发布和临时代码带来的风险。\r\n\r\nI worked with the team to divide responsibilities clearly.\r\n\r\n我和团队一起明确了分工和责任。\r\n\r\nThis helped the team work together instead of handling each urgent request independently.\r\n\r\n这帮助团队协作处理问题，而不是每个人独立处理紧急需求。\r\n\r\nWe made a practical decision based on the available information: temporary requirements used configuration, while long-term features still followed the normal design process.','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 06:54:39',1,'2026-04-26 06:54:39'),(39,1,'10. How do you encourage others to take responsibility and be\r\naccountable?\r\nGive me a specific example.\r\nHow did the person and task align?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','10. 你是如何鼓励他人承担责任并对结果负责的？\r\n请给一个具体例子。\r\n这个人和这个任务是如何匹配的？\r\n最终结果是什么？\r\n更自然的中文理解是：\r\n请举一个例子，说明你如何帮助或推动团队成员承担某项任务，并对任务结果负责。你为什么认为这个人适合做这件事？你是如何支持他的？最后结果怎么样？','I encourage others to take responsibility by setting clear ownership, matching tasks with the right people, and making sure risks are discussed early.\r\n\r\nFor example, in my previous role at China Unicom Online Information Technology, I worked on a high-volume email and SMS delivery system integrated with the three major telecom carriers. The system had several important modules, such as message intake, Kafka-based asynchronous processing, carrier gateway integration, retry handling, delivery status tracking, and logging.\r\n\r\nDuring the project, I did not simply divide the work equally. Instead, I considered each team member’s strengths. One colleague had good experience with external API integration and exception handling, so I suggested that he take ownership of the carrier gateway integration and retry mechanism.\r\n\r\nThe person and the task aligned well because the task required careful handling of timeouts, rate limits, failed requests, retry logic, and different response codes from carriers. I also made sure he understood the acceptance criteria, such as whether failed messages could be retried correctly, whether failure reasons were logged properly, and whether delivery status updates could be tracked without data loss.\r\n\r\nDuring development, when carrier interface rules changed or some response codes were not covered in testing, we discussed the changes together and adjusted the solution. I encouraged the team to raise risks early instead of waiting until the end of the sprint. This helped everyone think as one team, rather than working as separate individuals.\r\n\r\nAs a result, the module was completed on time and successfully integrated into the overall delivery system. More importantly, the team member developed stronger ownership of his work, and the team reduced delivery risks through early communication and clear accountability.','我鼓励他人承担责任的方式，主要是 明确责任归属、把合适的任务交给合适的人，并且提前讨论潜在风险。\r\n\r\n比如，在我之前中国联通在线信息科技的工作中，我参与过一个 高并发邮件和短信发送系统。这个系统需要对接三大运营商，处理大量邮件和短信发送请求，对系统稳定性、消息送达率和数据一致性要求都很高。\r\n\r\n在这个项目中，我们有多个模块需要开发，比如消息接收、异步处理、运营商网关对接、失败重试、发送状态追踪和日志监控。我没有简单地平均分配任务，而是根据每个同事的经验和优势来安排工作。\r\n\r\n其中一位同事比较熟悉外部 API 对接和异常处理，所以我建议他负责 运营商网关对接和失败重试机制 这一部分。这个任务和他的能力很匹配，因为它需要处理接口超时、运营商限流、发送失败、重试逻辑，以及不同运营商返回码的解析。\r\n\r\n为了让他真正对这部分工作负责，我和他一起明确了验收标准。例如，消息发送失败后是否能够正确重试，失败原因是否能被准确记录，发送状态是否能够被完整追踪，以及是否能避免消息丢失或重复发送。\r\n\r\n在开发过程中，如果运营商接口规则发生变化，或者测试中发现某些返回码没有覆盖，我们会及时一起讨论并调整方案。我也鼓励他和团队成员尽早暴露问题，而不是等到项目后期才发现风险。这样大家虽然各自有明确的 ownership，但仍然是作为一个团队来保证系统稳定交付。\r\n\r\n最终，这个模块按时完成，并成功集成到整个消息发送系统中。更重要的是，这位同事对自己的模块有了更强的责任感，团队也通过提前沟通和明确责任降低了交付风险。','1. How do you encourage others to take responsibility and be accountable?\r\n你回答的是：\r\n\r\nI set clear ownership, match the right task with the right person, and discuss risks early.\r\n\r\n也就是：\r\n明确责任 + 合适的人做合适的事 + 提前暴露风险。\r\n\r\n2. Give me a specific example.\r\n你用了简历里的真实项目：\r\n\r\nhigh-volume email and SMS delivery system integrated with the three major telecom carriers\r\n\r\n这个比普通学生项目更有说服力，因为它体现了你高级工程师经验。\r\n\r\n3. How did the person and task align?\r\n你说：\r\n\r\nOne colleague had experience with external API integration and exception handling, so he took ownership of carrier gateway integration and retry mechanism.\r\n\r\n这就回答了：\r\n这个人为什么适合这个任务。\r\n\r\n4. What was the outcome?\r\n你说：\r\n\r\nThe module was completed on time, integrated successfully, and the team reduced delivery risks.\r\n\r\n结果包含三层：\r\n\r\n* 任务按时完成\r\n* 模块成功集成\r\n* 团队责任感和风险意识提升\r\n\r\nTips：面试时一定要强调的关键词\r\n\r\n你可以重点记这些表达：\r\n\r\nClear ownership\r\n明确责任归属\r\n\r\nMatch the task with the right person\r\n把任务和合适的人匹配起来\r\n\r\nAcceptance criteria\r\n验收标准\r\n\r\nRaise risks early\r\n提前暴露风险\r\n\r\nRetry mechanism\r\n重试机制\r\n\r\nDelivery status tracking\r\n发送状态追踪\r\n\r\nAvoid data loss\r\n避免数据丢失\r\n\r\nWork as one team\r\n作为一个团队协作\r\n\r\nReduce delivery risk\r\n降低交付风险','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 07:13:35',1,'2026-04-26 07:13:35'),(40,1,'11. How do you ensure that you take all available information into account\r\nin assessing risk?\r\nGive me a specific example.\r\nWhat information did you look to?\r\nWhat was the outcome？','11. 你如何确保在评估风险时，把所有可用的信息都考虑进去？\r\n请给我一个具体例子。\r\n你查看或参考了哪些信息？\r\n最后结果是什么？','I ensure that I take all available information into account by collecting information from different sources, checking both technical and business impacts, and discussing the risks with relevant team members before making a decision.\r\n\r\nFor example, in my previous role at China Unicom Online Information Technology, I worked on the refactoring of a legacy Product Configuration System. Because this system was connected to multiple business processes, any change could affect production data and downstream services.\r\n\r\nBefore assessing the risk, I looked at several types of information. First, I reviewed the existing system logic and database structure to understand how the old system worked. Second, I checked production data patterns to identify high-risk scenarios, such as inconsistent product configurations or missing mappings. Third, I discussed with business stakeholders and other developers to understand which functions were most critical. I also considered technical risks such as data inconsistency, service downtime, and rollback difficulty.\r\n\r\nBased on this information, we decided to use a grey release and dual-write strategy. This allowed the old and new systems to run in parallel for a period of time, so we could compare data results and reduce the risk of a sudden full migration.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was positive. The system was migrated without major production incidents, and we achieved a smoother transition with minimal impact on users. This experience showed me that good risk assessment depends on looking at both technical evidence and business context, not just one source of information.','我在评估风险时，会尽量从不同来源收集信息，同时考虑技术影响和业务影响，而不是只根据个人经验或感觉来判断。\r\n\r\n比如，我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与过一个 老旧产品配置系统的重构项目。这个系统和多个业务流程相关联，所以任何改动都可能影响生产数据和下游服务。\r\n\r\n在评估风险之前，我主要查看了几类信息。第一，我分析了原系统的业务逻辑和数据库结构，了解旧系统是如何运行的。第二，我查看了生产数据的特点，识别高风险场景，比如产品配置不一致、数据映射缺失等问题。第三，我和业务方以及其他开发人员沟通，确认哪些功能是最关键、最不能出问题的。同时，我也考虑了技术风险，比如数据不一致、服务中断，以及如果出问题是否容易回滚。\r\n\r\n基于这些信息，我们最终采用了 灰度发布和双写策略。也就是说，让新旧系统在一段时间内并行运行，同时写入数据，并对比结果。这样可以避免一次性切换带来的高风险，也能及时发现数据差异。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好，系统迁移过程中没有出现严重的生产事故，用户受到的影响也很小。这个经历让我认识到，好的风险评估不能只看技术问题，也要结合业务场景、生产数据和团队反馈来综合判断。','1. 先说你的方法\r\n\r\n不要一开始就讲例子，可以先说你的风险评估方法：\r\n\r\nI collect information from different sources and consider both technical and business impacts.\r\n\r\n中文意思：\r\n\r\n我会从不同来源收集信息，并同时考虑技术影响和业务影响。\r\n\r\n\r\n2. 例子最好用“系统重构 / 迁移 / 灰度发布”\r\n\r\n你简历里的这个项目非常适合：\r\n\r\nRefactoring of legacy Product Configuration System\r\nGrey release and dual-write data strategies\r\nZero-downtime transitions\r\n\r\n这些词很适合回答风险评估类问题。\r\n\r\n\r\n3. 必须回答 “What information did you look to?”\r\n\r\n你可以列出 4 类信息：\r\n\r\nExisting system logic\r\n旧系统逻辑\r\n\r\nDatabase structure and data dependencies\r\n数据库结构和数据依赖\r\n\r\nProduction data patterns\r\n生产数据特点\r\n\r\nBusiness stakeholder feedback\r\n业务方反馈\r\n\r\nRollback plan and technical constraints\r\n回滚方案和技术限制\r\n\r\n4. 必须体现 “risk mitigation”\r\n\r\n也就是你收集信息之后，做了什么来降低风险。\r\n\r\n这里可以说：\r\n\r\nWe used a grey release and dual-write strategy.\r\n\r\n中文：\r\n\r\n我们采用了灰度发布和双写策略。\r\n\r\n这比只说 “we tested carefully” 更专业。\r\n\r\n\r\n5. outcome 要具体\r\n\r\n结果不要只说 “it was successful”。\r\n\r\n可以说：\r\n\r\nThe migration was completed without major production incidents and with minimal impact on users.\r\n\r\n中文：\r\n\r\n系统迁移没有出现严重生产事故，对用户影响很小。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 07:17:06',1,'2026-04-26 07:17:06'),(41,1,'12. What do you believe makes a team effective and why has this worked\r\nwell?\r\nWhy do you think that these things are so important?\r\nTell me about a time when you were in a team where things weren’t going\r\nwell.\r\nWhat did you do to improve the situation?','12. 你认为是什么让一个团队变得高效？为什么这些因素能发挥作用？\r\n\r\n为什么你认为这些因素这么重要？\r\n\r\n请讲一次你所在的团队运作不太顺利的经历。\r\n\r\n你做了什么来改善这种情况？','I believe an effective team needs clear goals, clear ownership, timely communication, and a shared awareness of risks. These things are important because in complex software projects, if people only focus on their own tasks without understanding the overall goal and dependencies, it can easily lead to duplicated work, integration issues, delays, or even production incidents.\r\n\r\nA specific example was a high-volume email and SMS delivery system I worked on at China Unicom Online Information Technology. The system was integrated with the three major telecom carriers and had high requirements for stability, delivery reliability, and data consistency. It included several modules, such as message intake, Kafka-based asynchronous processing, carrier gateway integration, retry handling, delivery status tracking, and logging.\r\n\r\nAt the beginning of the project, the teamwork was not going very smoothly. Some team members focused mainly on their own modules and did not have a clear understanding of the whole message delivery process. For example, carrier integration, retry logic, and delivery status tracking were closely connected. If each person worked too independently, we could face risks such as inconsistent message status, uncovered failure scenarios, or unclear accountability during integration.\r\n\r\nTo improve the situation, I first worked with the team to map out the complete message delivery flow, including the input, output, and dependencies of each module. Then I suggested task ownership based on each colleague’s strengths. For example, a colleague who had experience with external API integration and exception handling took ownership of the carrier gateway integration and retry mechanism. Another colleague who was stronger in data processing focused on delivery status tracking and logging.\r\n\r\nI also encouraged the team to raise risks early instead of waiting until the final integration stage. For example, when carrier interface rules changed, response codes were unclear, or some failure scenarios were not covered, we discussed the risks together and adjusted the solution. This helped the team think of themselves as one team rather than separate individuals working independently.\r\n\r\nAs a result, the team’s collaboration improved significantly. Responsibilities became clearer, risks were identified earlier, and integration issues were reduced. The system was successfully integrated and delivered. This experience showed me that an effective team is not just about dividing tasks. It is about assigning the right people to the right tasks, creating clear accountability, and making sure everyone understands the shared goal and potential risks.','我认为一个高效的团队需要具备几个关键点：清晰的目标、明确的责任分工、及时的沟通，以及共同面对风险的意识。这些因素很重要，因为在复杂的软件项目中，如果每个人只关注自己的任务，而不理解整体目标和潜在风险，就很容易出现重复工作、接口不一致、延期，甚至生产问题。\r\n\r\n一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的高并发邮件和短信发送系统。这个系统需要对接三大运营商，对稳定性、消息送达率和数据一致性要求都很高。项目中涉及多个模块，比如消息接收、Kafka 异步处理、运营商网关对接、失败重试、发送状态追踪和日志监控。\r\n\r\n项目初期，团队的协作并不是特别顺畅。有些同事更关注自己负责的模块，缺少对整体流程的理解。例如，接口对接、重试机制和状态回执之间存在依赖关系，如果每个人只独立开发自己的部分，后期集成时就可能出现消息状态不一致、异常场景没有覆盖，或者问题责任不清楚的风险。\r\n\r\n为了改善这个情况，我做了几件事。首先，我和团队一起梳理了完整的消息发送流程，明确每个模块的输入、输出和依赖关系。其次，我根据同事的经验和优势来建议任务分工。例如，熟悉外部 API 和异常处理的同事负责运营商网关对接和失败重试机制；更熟悉数据处理的同事负责状态追踪和日志记录。这样任务和人员能力更加匹配，也更容易建立 ownership。\r\n\r\n同时，我也鼓励大家提前暴露风险，而不是等到最后集成时才发现问题。比如，如果运营商接口规则变化、返回码不清楚，或者某些失败场景没有覆盖，我们会及时一起讨论并调整方案。这样团队不是各自独立工作，而是把自己看作一个整体，共同保证系统稳定交付。\r\n\r\n最终，团队协作明显改善。各模块的责任更加清晰，问题能够更早被发现和解决，系统也顺利完成集成和交付。这个经历让我认识到，高效团队不是简单地把任务分给每个人，而是要让合适的人负责合适的任务，同时让大家对整体目标和风险有共同理解。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 07:29:10',1,'2026-04-26 07:29:10'),(42,2,'13. How do you ensure that customer needs are met?\r\nGive me a specific example.\r\nWhat feedback have you received from your internal customers?\r\nYour external customer?','13. 你如何确保客户需求得到满足？\r\n请给我一个具体例子。\r\n你从内部客户那里收到过什么反馈？\r\n你从外部客户那里收到过什么反馈？','I ensure customer needs are met by first understanding their real requirements, then translating those requirements into clear technical solutions, and finally checking the quality carefully before delivery. I also pay close attention to feedback and use it to improve the system rather than becoming defensive.\r\n\r\nA specific example was a high-volume email and SMS delivery system I worked on at China Unicom Online Information Technology. The system supported business notifications such as billing reminders and service messages, so reliability and delivery status tracking were very important to both internal and external customers.\r\n\r\nOur internal customers included business teams, operations teams, and other internal systems that needed to send messages through our platform. Their needs were mainly stability, clear delivery status, easy integration, and fast problem investigation. To meet these needs, I worked with the team to clarify the requirements, such as message retry rules, delivery status callbacks, logging, and monitoring. I also made sure we checked for errors carefully, especially around failed messages, duplicate sending, and missing status updates.\r\n\r\nFor external customers and third-party systems, one common need was easier integration. To support them, we developed robust SDKs and clear API documentation, so they could connect to our messaging service more easily and reduce integration errors.\r\n\r\nThe feedback from internal customers was that the system became more stable and easier to monitor. Operations teams could identify failed messages more quickly, and business teams had better visibility of message delivery status. From external customers and third-party users, the feedback was that the API and SDK made integration clearer and more reliable.\r\n\r\nThis experience showed me that meeting customer needs is not only about delivering features. It is also about listening carefully, providing useful information, checking the quality of work, and continuously improving the service based on feedback.','我确保客户需求被满足的方式，主要是 先理解客户真正的需求，再把需求转化成清晰的技术方案，最后在交付前认真检查质量。同时，我也会认真接受客户反馈，并把反馈作为改进系统的机会，而不是进行防御性回应。\r\n\r\n一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的 高并发邮件和短信发送系统。这个系统主要支持账单提醒、服务通知等业务消息，所以系统的稳定性、消息送达率和发送状态追踪都非常重要。\r\n\r\n我们的 内部客户 包括业务团队、运营团队、客服团队，以及其他需要调用我们消息平台的内部系统。他们的核心需求是系统稳定、状态清晰、方便排查问题，并且能够快速集成。为了满足这些需求，我和团队一起明确了关键功能，比如消息失败后的重试规则、发送状态回调、日志记录和监控告警。同时，我也会特别关注质量检查，避免出现消息丢失、重复发送、失败状态没有记录等问题。\r\n\r\n对于 外部客户或第三方系统 来说，他们比较关注的是接口是否稳定、是否容易接入、文档是否清晰。为了支持他们，我们开发了可靠的 SDK，并完善了 API 文档，让第三方系统可以更容易地接入我们的邮件和短信发送服务，也减少了集成过程中的错误。\r\n\r\n我们收到的内部客户反馈是，系统上线后稳定性更好，运营团队可以更快定位失败消息，业务团队也能更清楚地看到消息发送状态。外部客户或第三方系统的反馈是，SDK 和 API 文档让接入过程更清晰、更可靠。\r\n\r\n这个经历让我认识到，以客户为中心不只是完成一个功能，而是要认真倾听客户需求，交付高质量的结果，主动提供对客户有用的信息，并根据客户反馈持续改进系统。','1. 先回答方法：怎么确保客户需求被满足\r\n\r\n可以说：\r\n\r\nI listen carefully to customer needs, clarify expectations, translate them into technical requirements, and check quality before delivery.\r\n\r\n中文意思：\r\n\r\n我会认真听客户需求，明确他们的期望，把它们转化成技术需求，并在交付前检查质量。\r\n\r\n\r\n2. 一定要区分 internal customers 和 external customers\r\n\r\n这个问题专门问了：\r\n\r\ninternal customers\r\nexternal customer\r\n\r\n你可以这样理解：\r\n\r\nInternal customers 内部客户：\r\n\r\n* 业务团队\r\n* 运营团队\r\n* 客服团队\r\n* 公司内部其他系统或开发团队\r\n\r\nExternal customers 外部客户：\r\n\r\n* 第三方接入方\r\n* 使用平台服务的合作方\r\n* 最终接收短信或邮件通知的用户\r\n\r\n\r\n3. 要体现 customer-centricity 的评分标准\r\n\r\n你需要覆盖这些点：\r\n\r\nPuts customers first\r\n把客户需求放在优先位置\r\n\r\nChecks for errors and rejects sub-standard work\r\n认真检查错误，不接受低质量交付\r\n\r\nAccepts customer feedback without being defensive\r\n接受客户反馈，不防御、不找借口\r\n\r\nPays close attention when customers are speaking\r\n认真倾听客户表达的需求\r\n\r\nMakes customers aware of useful information\r\n主动提供对客户有用的信息，比如状态、文档、日志、监控结果','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 07:32:12',1,'2026-04-26 07:32:12'),(43,1,'14. Give me an example of when you have assessed quality and customer\r\nsatisfaction.\r\nWhat was the context of this?\r\nHow did you approach your task?\r\nWhat was the outcome?','14. 请举一个你评估质量和客户满意度的例子。\r\n当时的背景是什么？\r\n你是如何处理这个任务的？\r\n最后的结果是什么？','One example was when I worked on a high-volume email and SMS delivery system at China Unicom Online Information Technology. The system supported business notifications such as billing reminders and service messages, so both quality and customer satisfaction were very important.\r\n\r\nThe context was that our internal customers, such as business teams and operations teams, needed the system to be stable, reliable, and easy to monitor. At the same time, external customers and third-party systems needed clear APIs and SDKs so they could integrate with our platform smoothly.\r\n\r\nTo assess quality, I looked at both technical indicators and customer feedback. From the technical side, I checked message delivery success rates, failed message records, retry results, duplicate sending risks, delivery status callbacks, logs, and monitoring alerts. I wanted to make sure the system was not only working, but working correctly and consistently.\r\n\r\nFrom the customer satisfaction side, I listened to feedback from internal users and third-party integration teams. For example, operations teams wanted clearer failure reasons so they could investigate issues faster. Third-party developers wanted better API documentation and more reliable SDK behaviour. I accepted this feedback and used it to improve the system instead of treating it as criticism.\r\n\r\nMy approach was to improve the retry mechanism, make delivery status tracking clearer, strengthen logging and monitoring, and improve the SDK and API documentation. I also encouraged the team to check edge cases carefully before release, especially around failed messages, timeout handling, and status updates.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was positive. The system became more stable and easier to monitor. Internal teams could identify problems more quickly, and third-party customers found the integration process clearer and more reliable. This experience showed me that assessing quality is not just about checking whether a feature works. It is also about understanding whether the customer can use it easily, reliably, and with confidence.','一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的 高并发邮件和短信发送系统。这个系统主要用于账单提醒、服务通知等业务场景，所以系统质量和客户满意度都非常重要。\r\n\r\n当时的背景是，我们的 内部客户，比如业务团队、运营团队和客服团队，需要这个系统稳定、可靠，并且方便监控和排查问题。同时，外部客户或第三方系统 也需要清晰的 API 和可靠的 SDK，这样他们才能顺利接入我们的邮件和短信发送平台。\r\n\r\n在评估质量时，我主要从两个方面入手。第一是技术质量指标，比如消息发送成功率、失败消息记录、重试结果、是否存在重复发送风险、发送状态回调是否完整、日志是否清晰、监控告警是否及时。我的目标不是只看功能能不能跑通，而是要确认系统是否能稳定、准确、一致地运行。\r\n\r\n第二是客户满意度。我会认真听取内部用户和第三方接入方的反馈。例如，运营团队希望失败原因更清楚，这样他们可以更快定位问题；第三方开发人员希望 API 文档更清晰，SDK 行为更稳定，减少接入过程中的错误。我会接受这些反馈，并把它们作为改进系统质量的依据，而不是把它们看作批评。\r\n\r\n我的处理方式是：优化失败重试机制，让发送状态追踪更清晰，加强日志记录和监控告警，同时改进 SDK 和 API 文档。我也会鼓励团队在发布前认真检查边界场景，尤其是发送失败、接口超时、状态回调丢失和重复发送等问题。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好。系统稳定性提升了，运营团队能够更快发现和定位问题，业务团队也能更清楚地看到消息发送状态。第三方客户反馈说，SDK 和 API 文档让接入过程更清晰、更可靠。这个经历让我认识到，评估质量不只是看功能有没有完成，还要看客户是否能稳定、方便、有信心地使用这个系统。','1. Puts customers first\r\n你体现了优先考虑客户需求：\r\n\r\ninternal customers needed stability and monitoring; external customers needed clear APIs and SDKs.\r\n\r\n2. Checks for errors and rejects sub-standard work\r\n你体现了质量检查：\r\n\r\ndelivery success rates, failed records, retry results, duplicate sending risks, callbacks, logs, and alerts.\r\n\r\n3. Accepts customer feedback without becoming defensive\r\n你体现了接受反馈：\r\n\r\nI accepted this feedback and used it to improve the system instead of treating it as criticism.\r\n\r\n\r\n4. Pays close attention to customers when they are speaking\r\n你体现了认真倾听：\r\n\r\noperations teams wanted clearer failure reasons; third-party developers wanted better API documentation.\r\n\r\n\r\n5. Makes customers aware of useful information\r\n你体现了给客户提供有用信息：\r\n\r\nclearer delivery status tracking, logs, monitoring, API documentation.','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:39:09',1,'2026-04-26 08:39:09'),(44,2,'15. When have you received negative/constructive feedback?\r\nWhat was the situation?\r\nHow did you react?\r\nWhat action did you take?','15. 当你收到负面的或者建设性的反馈的具体情况\r\n你是怎么回应的\r\n你采取了什么措施','One example was when I worked on the refactoring of a legacy cloud storage product system at China Unicom Online Information Technology.\r\n\r\nThe situation was that the old system had been used for a long time, and it supported several product configuration processes, such as product packages, pricing rules, user entitlements, and service activation. During the refactoring project, our internal customers included product managers, operations teams, and customer service teams.\r\n\r\nAfter the first version of the refactored system was delivered, we received some constructive feedback from the operations team. They said the new system was technically working, but some configuration steps were not clear enough for daily operations. For example, when they configured a new cloud storage package, they wanted clearer validation messages, better visibility of configuration status, and more information about whether the configuration had been successfully synchronised to downstream systems.\r\n\r\nI did not become defensive or simply explain that the system logic was correct. Instead, I listened carefully and asked them to walk me through their daily workflow. I realised that although the backend functions were working, the system still needed to provide clearer information to users, especially when there were validation errors or synchronisation delays.\r\n\r\nAfter that, I took several actions. I reviewed the configuration workflow with the product and operations teams, improved validation rules and error messages, added clearer status indicators, and enhanced logs so that operations staff could check synchronisation results more easily. I also worked with the team to improve the release plan using a grey release and dual-write strategy, so we could reduce migration risks while continuing to collect feedback.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was positive. The operations team found the new system easier to use and more transparent. They could identify configuration issues earlier, reduce manual checking, and feel more confident using the refactored system. This experience taught me that constructive feedback is not just about fixing defects. It is also a way to improve usability, quality, and customer satisfaction.','一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的 云盘产品系统重构项目。\r\n\r\n当时的情况是，旧系统已经使用了比较长时间，主要支持云盘产品的配置流程，比如产品套餐、价格规则、用户权益、服务开通等。这个系统的内部客户主要包括产品经理、运营团队和客服团队。\r\n\r\n在重构系统的第一个版本交付后，我们收到了一些来自运营团队的建设性反馈。他们认为新系统在技术功能上是可以运行的，但是在日常配置操作中，有些步骤还不够清晰。例如，当他们配置一个新的云盘套餐时，希望系统能提供更明确的校验提示、更清楚的配置状态，以及更直观的信息来确认配置是否已经成功同步到下游系统。\r\n\r\n当时我没有防御性地解释“系统逻辑是正确的”或者“功能已经实现了”。相反，我认真听取了他们的反馈，并请他们按照日常工作流程演示一遍操作过程。通过这个过程，我意识到，虽然后端功能是正常的，但从客户使用角度来看，系统还需要提供更多清晰、有用的信息，尤其是在校验失败、配置状态不明确，或者下游同步有延迟的情况下。\r\n\r\n之后，我采取了几项改进措施。首先，我和产品、运营团队一起重新梳理了配置流程。然后，我们优化了字段校验规则和错误提示，让运营人员更容易理解问题原因。同时，我们增加了更清楚的状态标识，增强了日志记录，让运营人员能够更方便地查看配置是否同步成功。另外，在系统发布方面，我们也结合灰度发布和双写策略，逐步切换新旧系统，降低重构上线带来的风险，并持续收集反馈。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好。运营团队反馈说，新系统比之前更容易使用，配置状态也更透明。他们可以更早发现配置问题，减少了人工核对的时间，也更有信心使用重构后的系统。这个经历让我认识到，建设性反馈不只是帮助我们修复缺陷，也能帮助我们提升系统可用性、交付质量和客户满意度。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:44:23',1,'2026-04-26 08:44:23'),(45,2,'16. Give me an example of where you shared knowledge or information\r\nwith a customer.\r\nWho exactly were you sharing the information with?\r\nHow did you share your expertise?\r\nHow did the others benefit from the interaction?','16. 举例说明你向你的客户分享知识和信息的场景\r\n对方是谁？\r\n你怎么体现出你的专业性\r\n从这次交流中你的收益是什么','One example was during the refactoring of a legacy cloud storage product system at China Unicom Online Information Technology.\r\n\r\nThe people I shared information with were mainly internal customers, including product managers and operations staff. They were responsible for configuring cloud storage product packages, pricing rules, user entitlements, and service activation rules. They were not all technical users, so they needed clear information about how the new system worked and how it was different from the old one.\r\n\r\nI shared my expertise in several ways. First, I explained the new configuration workflow in simple language, focusing on what had changed and what they needed to pay attention to in daily operations. Second, I prepared clear examples, such as how to configure a new cloud storage package, how to check whether the configuration had been synchronised to downstream systems, and how to understand validation messages. Third, during the grey release period, I supported them by answering questions and explaining common issues when they appeared.\r\n\r\nI also made sure the information was useful from their point of view. For example, instead of only explaining backend logic, I showed them how to check configuration status, where to find error messages, and when they should contact the technical team. This helped them feel more confident using the new system.\r\n\r\nThe benefit was that the product and operations teams understood the new system more clearly and made fewer configuration mistakes. They were able to identify problems earlier, reduce manual checking, and use the system more independently. It also reduced repeated support questions for the development team and made the transition from the old system to the new system smoother.','一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的 云盘产品系统重构项目。\r\n\r\n我分享信息的对象主要是 内部客户，包括产品经理和运营人员。他们负责配置云盘产品套餐、价格规则、用户权益和服务开通规则。因为他们并不都是技术人员，所以他们需要清楚了解新系统如何使用，以及新系统和旧系统相比有哪些变化。\r\n\r\n我主要通过几种方式分享我的专业知识。首先，我用比较容易理解的语言向他们解释新的配置流程，重点说明哪些地方发生了变化，以及他们在日常操作中需要注意什么。其次，我准备了一些具体例子，比如如何配置一个新的云盘套餐，如何查看配置是否已经同步到下游系统，以及如何理解系统中的校验提示。第三，在灰度发布期间，我也会及时回答他们的问题，并解释一些常见问题的原因和处理方式。\r\n\r\n我特别注意从客户的角度提供有用信息，而不是只讲后端技术逻辑。例如，我不会只说“数据已经双写成功”或者“接口调用正常”，而是会告诉他们在哪里查看配置状态，如何判断同步是否成功，遇到错误提示时应该先检查哪些信息，以及什么时候需要联系技术团队。\r\n\r\n最终的效果比较好。产品和运营团队对新系统的理解更清楚，配置错误减少了，也能更早发现问题。他们不再完全依赖开发人员，可以更独立地完成日常配置工作。同时，开发团队也减少了很多重复性的支持问题，整个系统从旧版本切换到新版本的过程更加顺畅。','1. Puts customers first\r\n你不是只讲技术，而是根据产品和运营团队的实际工作需求来分享信息。\r\n\r\n2. Makes customers aware of useful information\r\n你主动告诉客户：\r\n\r\n* 如何配置产品套餐\r\n* 如何查看同步状态\r\n* 如何理解错误提示\r\n* 什么时候联系技术团队\r\n\r\n3. Pays close attention to customers when they are speaking\r\n你根据他们“不太理解新流程”“不确定同步状态”的反馈，提供更清楚的说明。\r\n\r\n4. Checks for errors and supports quality\r\n通过知识分享减少配置错误，提高系统使用质量。','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:47:19',1,'2026-04-26 08:47:19'),(46,2,'17. Give me an example of a situation which demonstrates your ability to\r\nthink laterally and broadly about your work.\r\nWhy did you need to think broadly?\r\nHow did your thinking style help?\r\nWhat was the outcome to this situation?','17. 请举一个例子，说明你能够对自己的工作进行横向思考和广泛思考。\r\n你为什么需要进行广泛思考？\r\n你的思考方式是如何起作用的？\r\n这个情况最后的结果是什么？','One example was when I worked at Maodou Mall on the backend architecture for a large-scale e-commerce application.\r\n\r\nAt first, the main problem looked like a technical issue. The existing system was becoming difficult to maintain, and new business requirements were taking longer to deliver. However, I realised we needed to think more broadly because the problem was not only about code quality. It also affected delivery speed, system performance, team collaboration, operational cost, and the company’s ability to respond to market changes.\r\n\r\nI needed to think broadly because e-commerce is a highly competitive environment. Product changes, marketing campaigns, and user-growth activities often require fast system support. If the backend architecture remained tightly coupled, every change would involve many dependencies and high regression risk. This would slow down delivery and increase testing and maintenance costs.\r\n\r\nMy thinking style helped because I looked at the issue from several angles. From a technical perspective, I considered service boundaries, system performance, caching strategy, and high-concurrency scenarios. From a business perspective, I considered how quickly the platform could support new features and promotions. From a team perspective, I considered whether different teams could work independently without blocking each other. From a cost perspective, I wanted to avoid unnecessary repeated development and reduce future maintenance effort.\r\n\r\nBased on this broader thinking, I helped decouple the monolithic system into several business domains and also independently engineered a configuration centre with three-level caching. The configuration centre helped reduce latency in high-concurrency scenarios and allowed product or business configuration changes to be managed more efficiently.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was positive. The new architecture improved system maintainability and performance, reduced unnecessary dependency between modules, and helped teams deliver changes more quickly. It also supported future business growth with lower maintenance cost. This experience showed me that enterprise thinking means considering not only the technical solution, but also business speed, cost, team efficiency, market competitiveness, and long-term value.','一个具体例子是我在 Maodou Mall 工作期间，参与一个大规模电商 App 后端架构设计和优化的项目。\r\n\r\n一开始，这个问题看起来只是一个技术问题。原来的系统越来越难维护，新业务需求交付速度也变慢。但是我意识到，我们需要从更广的角度去思考，因为这个问题不只是代码质量问题，它还会影响业务交付速度、系统性能、团队协作、运营成本，以及公司响应市场变化的能力。\r\n\r\n我之所以需要进行广泛思考，是因为电商行业竞争非常激烈。产品功能调整、营销活动、用户增长活动通常都需要系统快速支持。如果后端系统仍然高度耦合，每次改动都会牵涉很多模块，回归测试风险也比较高。这样不仅会拖慢交付速度，还会增加测试成本和后期维护成本。\r\n\r\n我的思考方式之所以有帮助，是因为我不是只从开发角度看问题，而是从多个角度进行判断。从技术角度，我考虑服务边界、系统性能、缓存策略和高并发场景；从业务角度，我考虑平台能否快速支持新功能和促销活动；从团队角度，我考虑不同团队是否可以相对独立开发，减少相互等待；从成本角度，我希望避免重复开发，降低未来维护成本。\r\n\r\n基于这种更全面的思考，我参与推动了系统从单体架构向多个业务领域服务拆分。同时，我也独立设计并实现了一个配置中心，并加入了三层缓存机制。这个配置中心可以在高并发场景下降低访问延迟，也让产品和业务配置变更更加高效。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好。新的架构提升了系统的可维护性和性能，减少了模块之间不必要的依赖，也让团队能够更快交付业务需求。同时，它也用更低的维护成本支持了后续业务增长。这个经历让我认识到，Enterprise thinking 不只是考虑技术方案本身，还要同时考虑业务速度、成本、团队效率、市场竞争力和长期价值。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:52:52',1,'2026-04-26 08:52:52'),(47,2,'18. What experience do you have of budgetary and cost control in your\r\nrole?\r\nWho sets the parameters?\r\nHow do you oversee the implementation?\r\n(for those with no direct experience : What part do you play in your team to\r\nminimise costs/overheads?)','18. 在你的岗位中，你有哪些预算管理和成本控制方面的经验？\r\n这些预算或成本控制的要求/参数是谁设定的？\r\n你是如何监督实施过程的？\r\n如果你没有直接的预算管理经验：你在团队中如何帮助降低成本或减少额外开销？','I have not directly owned a department budget or approved financial spending, but as a senior software engineer, I have played an important role in cost control by reducing technical overhead, improving system performance, and avoiding unnecessary operational and maintenance costs.\r\n\r\nOne example was when I worked at Maodou Mall on the backend services for a large-scale e-commerce application. The system had been split into multiple business domains, but some shared configuration data was still accessed frequently by many services. If every service queried the database directly, it would increase database pressure, slow down response time, and potentially require more infrastructure resources.\r\n\r\nThe broader cost-control parameters were usually set by the business and technical leadership, such as improving system performance, supporting high concurrency, reducing unnecessary infrastructure pressure, and improving delivery efficiency without creating extra operational cost.\r\n\r\nMy role was to design and implement a more efficient technical solution. I independently engineered a configuration centre with three-level caching, so frequently used configuration data could be accessed more efficiently. I also reviewed performance bottlenecks, checked cache hit rates, monitored database load, and worked with colleagues to make sure the solution was implemented consistently across services.\r\n\r\nThis helped reduce repeated database queries, lower system latency, and avoid unnecessary pressure on infrastructure. It also reduced future maintenance overhead because configuration logic was centralised rather than duplicated across multiple services.\r\n\r\nThe outcome was positive. The system became more stable in high-concurrency scenarios, the team avoided unnecessary infrastructure expansion, and developers could reuse the configuration centre instead of repeatedly building similar logic. This experience shows that even without direct budget ownership, I can contribute to cost control by making practical technical decisions that improve efficiency and reduce waste.','我没有直接负责部门预算，也没有审批财务支出的权限。但是作为高级软件工程师，我在成本控制方面的作用主要体现在：通过合理的技术方案减少系统资源浪费、降低维护成本、减少重复开发，并提升系统性能和交付效率。\r\n\r\n一个具体例子是我在 Maodou Mall 工作期间，参与大规模电商 App 后端服务的架构优化。当时系统已经拆分成多个业务领域，但是一些公共配置数据被很多服务频繁访问。如果每个服务都直接查询数据库，就会增加数据库压力，降低响应速度，在高并发场景下甚至可能需要更多服务器资源来支撑。\r\n\r\n整体的成本控制目标通常是由业务负责人和技术负责人共同设定的，比如提升系统性能、支持高并发、减少不必要的基础设施压力，并在不增加额外运营成本的情况下提高交付效率。\r\n\r\n我的角色是设计并实施更高效的技术方案。我独立设计并实现了一个 配置中心，并加入三层缓存机制。这样高频访问的配置数据可以通过缓存快速读取，而不是每次都访问数据库。同时，我也会关注性能瓶颈、缓存命中率、数据库负载，以及各个服务是否按照统一方式接入配置中心。\r\n\r\n这个方案帮助我们减少了重复数据库查询，降低了系统响应延迟，也避免了不必要的基础设施压力。同时，因为配置逻辑被集中管理，而不是分散在多个服务中，也减少了后续维护成本和重复开发成本。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好。系统在高并发场景下更加稳定，团队也避免了因为数据库压力过大而盲目增加服务器资源。开发人员可以复用配置中心，而不用在不同服务里重复实现类似逻辑。这个经历让我认识到，即使没有直接管理预算，工程师也可以通过合理的技术设计，帮助团队降低成本、减少浪费，并提高整体效率。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:55:40',1,'2026-04-26 08:55:40'),(48,2,'19. Describe your understanding of our competitors?\r\nWhat do you think they do better than us?\r\nHow could we be more successful?\r\nWhat opportunities do you see for new business in this area?','19. 请描述一下你对我们竞争对手的理解。\r\n你认为他们在哪些方面比我们做得更好？\r\n我们怎样才能更成功？\r\n你认为这个领域有哪些新的业务机会？','My understanding is that competitors should not only be seen as companies offering similar products or services, but also as organisations that may solve the same customer problems in a different way.\r\n\r\nFor example, in a technology or digital-service business, competitors may do better in areas such as user experience, pricing flexibility, faster delivery, stronger automation, or easier integration with other systems. Some competitors may also respond to market changes more quickly because their products are more modular or their internal processes are more agile.\r\n\r\nI think we could be more successful by focusing on three areas. First, we should understand customer needs more deeply and make sure our products solve real pain points, not just technical requirements. Second, we should improve operational efficiency and avoid unnecessary costs, for example by automating repeated work, improving system reliability, and reducing manual support. Third, we should build stronger collaboration between product, engineering, sales, and customer support teams, so that customer feedback can be turned into product improvements more quickly.\r\n\r\nIn terms of new business opportunities, I think there are opportunities in areas such as automation, data-driven services, AI-assisted customer support, and better integration platforms. Many customers want solutions that are reliable, easy to use, and easy to connect with their existing systems. If we can provide a service that reduces their operational workload and helps them make decisions faster, that could create strong business value.\r\n\r\nFrom my own software engineering background, I believe technical decisions should always support business goals. For example, building scalable architecture, reusable components, and clear APIs can help the organisation respond faster to market changes and compete more effectively.','我的理解是，竞争对手不只是指提供相似产品或服务的公司，也包括那些用不同方式解决同一类客户问题的企业。\r\n\r\n比如在技术或数字化服务领域，竞争对手可能在用户体验、价格灵活性、交付速度、自动化能力，或者和其他系统集成的便利性方面做得更好。有些竞争对手响应市场变化也更快，因为他们的产品更加模块化，内部流程也更加敏捷。\r\n\r\n我认为我们可以从三个方面变得更成功。第一，要更深入地理解客户需求，确保产品真正解决客户痛点，而不只是满足技术需求。第二，要提升运营效率，避免不必要的成本，比如通过自动化减少重复工作，提高系统稳定性，减少人工支持成本。第三，要加强产品、技术、销售和客服团队之间的协作，让客户反馈可以更快转化为产品改进。\r\n\r\n在新的业务机会方面，我认为可以关注自动化、数据驱动服务、AI 辅助客服，以及更好的系统集成平台。很多客户现在需要的是稳定、易用，并且容易和现有系统连接的解决方案。如果我们能提供一种服务，帮助客户减少运营工作量，并更快做出决策，就能创造很强的业务价值。\r\n\r\n结合我自己的软件工程背景，我认为技术决策始终应该服务于业务目标。比如，构建可扩展的架构、可复用的组件和清晰的 API，可以帮助企业更快响应市场变化，并提升竞争力。','这个问题不要回答得太绝对，比如：\r\n\r\nOur competitors are much better than us.\r\n\r\n这样不太好。\r\n\r\n更好的说法是：\r\n\r\nCompetitors may have strengths in some areas, such as user experience, automation, or speed of delivery.\r\n\r\n意思是：\r\n\r\n竞争对手可能在某些方面有优势，比如用户体验、自动化或交付速度。\r\n\r\n你要覆盖的 4 个评分点\r\n\r\n1. broad range of issues\r\n不要只说技术，要说：\r\n\r\n* customer needs\r\n* cost\r\n* market trends\r\n* user experience\r\n* product integration\r\n* operational efficiency\r\n\r\n2. avoid unnecessary costs\r\n要提到：\r\n\r\n* automation\r\n* reducing manual work\r\n* improving reliability\r\n* reusable components\r\n\r\n3. competitors and market trends\r\n要提到：\r\n\r\n* competitors may move faster\r\n* better user experience\r\n* easier integration\r\n* more flexible pricing\r\n\r\n4. relationships / collaboration\r\n要提到：\r\n\r\n* product team\r\n* engineering team\r\n* sales team\r\n* customer support team\r\n* customer feedback loop','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 08:59:02',1,'2026-04-26 08:59:02'),(49,1,'20. Tell me about a time when you have shown an ability to network.\r\nWhy did you need to do this?\r\nHow did you go about it?\r\nWhat was the result?','20. 请讲一次你展现 networking / 建立人际关系能力的经历。\r\n你为什么需要这样做？\r\n你是如何做的？\r\n结果是什么？','One example was during the refactoring of a legacy cloud storage product configuration system at China Unicom Online Information Technology.\r\n\r\nI needed to network because the system was not used by only one team. It was connected to product packages, pricing rules, user entitlements, service activation, operations support, and several downstream systems. If I only worked within the development team, we might miss important business rules or integration risks.\r\n\r\nTo do this, I actively built working relationships with product managers, operations staff, testers, and developers from downstream system teams. First, I spoke with product managers to understand the business rules behind cloud storage packages and pricing. Then I worked with operations staff to understand their daily configuration process and the problems they faced in the old system. I also communicated with downstream system teams to confirm data dependencies and integration requirements.\r\n\r\nDuring the project, I organised discussions when there were unclear requirements or potential risks. For example, when we planned the grey release and dual-write strategy, I made sure different teams understood how the old and new systems would run in parallel, how data would be compared, and how issues would be escalated.\r\n\r\nThe result was positive. We identified several risks earlier, reduced misunderstandings between teams, and made the migration smoother. The operations team understood the new system better, and the development team had clearer requirements and fewer late-stage changes. This experience showed me that networking is not just about knowing people. It is about building useful working relationships that help the team deliver better outcomes.','一个具体例子是我之前在中国联通在线信息科技参与的 云盘产品配置系统重构项目。\r\n\r\n我当时需要建立和利用工作关系，是因为这个系统并不是只影响开发团队。它还关联到产品套餐、价格规则、用户权益、服务开通、运营支持，以及多个下游系统。如果我只和开发团队内部沟通，很可能会遗漏重要的业务规则或集成风险。\r\n\r\n所以我主动和不同团队建立联系，包括产品经理、运营人员、测试人员，以及下游系统的开发团队。首先，我和产品经理沟通，了解云盘套餐和价格规则背后的业务逻辑。然后，我和运营人员沟通，了解他们日常配置产品时的流程，以及旧系统中经常遇到的问题。同时，我也和下游系统团队确认数据依赖和接口集成要求。\r\n\r\n在项目过程中，如果需求不清楚或者存在潜在风险，我会主动组织讨论。比如在设计灰度发布和双写策略时，我会确保相关团队都理解新旧系统如何并行运行、数据如何对比、如果出现问题应该如何升级处理。\r\n\r\n最终结果比较好。我们更早识别了一些风险，减少了团队之间的误解，也让系统迁移过程更加顺利。运营团队对新系统的理解更清楚，开发团队也获得了更明确的需求，减少了后期返工和临时变更。这个经历让我认识到，networking 不只是认识很多人，而是建立有效的工作关系，帮助自己和团队更好地完成工作。','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-04-26 09:02:39',1,'2026-04-27 15:49:12'),(50,2,'One-minute self-introduction','一分钟自我介绍','I’m a Senior Software Engineer with over 15 years of experience designing and building scalable backend systems across finance, e-commerce, and telecommunications.\r\n\r\nThroughout my career, I’ve focused on system reliability, microservices architecture, and performance optimization. I’ve worked extensively with Java and Python on the backend, and I’m also comfortable building modern front-end interfaces using React. I have experience with distributed systems, CI/CD pipelines, and cloud-based deployments.\r\n\r\nAfter working in China for many years, I decided to come to New Zealand to study and broaden my international experience. Studying here has helped me improve my English, understand the local culture better, and adapt to a more diverse working and learning environment.\r\n\r\nIn recent years, I’ve been particularly interested in building high-throughput, low-latency systems and ensuring clean, maintainable architecture that supports long-term growth.\r\n\r\nI value collaboration, clear communication, and engineering quality. I’m excited about the opportunity to contribute my experience while continuing to learn and grow with your team.\r\n\r\nThank you for considering my application.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-05-09 14:29:45',1,'2026-05-09 14:29:45'),(51,2,'Why did you come here to work?','为什么要来新西兰工作','I moved to New Zealand because I wanted to broaden my career experience and work in a more international environment. I’ve worked in China for many years, mainly in backend systems and distributed systems, so I hope to bring that experience here and also learn from the local industry and culture.','','','',1,0,0,1,1,'2026-05-09 14:32:10',1,'2026-05-09 14:32:10');
